AIMED.سجّل({"slug":"renal-system","عنوان":"Renal and Urinary System","تمهيد":"","أقسام":[{"معرّف":"first-symptoms-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"First: Symptoms of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>Hematuria (Gross and Microscopic)</li><li>Proteinuria and Frothy Urine</li><li>Edema (Periorbital, Peripheral, Anasarca)</li><li>Oliguria and Anuria</li><li>Polyuria</li><li>Nocturia</li><li>Dysuria</li><li>Urinary Frequency and Urgency</li><li>Flank Pain and Loin Pain</li><li>Uremic Symptoms (Fatigue, Anorexia, Nausea, Vomiting, Pruritus, Sleep Disturbance, Dysgeusia, Restless Legs)</li><li>Urinary Hesitancy, Poor Stream, Intermittency, Terminal Dribbling</li><li>Pneumaturia and Fecaluria</li><li>Urethral Discharge</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"second-signs-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Second: Signs of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Pallor</li><li>Hyperpigmentation (Uremic Pigmentation)</li><li>Asterixis</li><li>Pericardial Friction Rub</li><li>Uremic Frost</li><li>Edema (Pitting, Ascites, Pleural Effusion)</li><li>Palpable Kidney and Abdominal Mass</li><li>Distended Bladder</li><li>Bladder Percussion Dullness</li><li>Costovertebral Angle (CVA) Tenderness</li><li>Pulmonary Crackles and Elevated Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP)</li><li>Cutaneous Signs (Petechiae, Purpura, Livedo Reticularis, Calciphylaxis, Nail Changes)</li><li>Arteriovenous Fistula Signs (Thrill, Bruit, Aneurysm, Infection, Steal Syndrome)</li><li>Fundoscopic Findings (Hypertensive Retinopathy, Diabetic Retinopathy)</li><li>Uremic Breath (Ammoniacal Odor, Fetor Uremicus)</li><li>Genital Edema</li><li>Prostatic Abnormalities on Digital Rectal Examination</li><li>Ascites</li><li>Xanthomata and Xanthelasma</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"third-clinical-examination-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Third: Clinical Examination of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>General Inspection and Volume Status Assessment (End of Bed)</li><li>Vital Signs (Blood Pressure, Heart Rate, Respiratory Rate, Temperature)</li><li>Skin Examination (Uremic Frost, Pruritus Marks, Calciphylaxis, Pallor, Bruising)</li><li>Face and Eyes (Periorbital Edema, Conjunctival Pallor, Malar Rash)</li><li>Cardiovascular Examination (JVP, Cardiac Auscultation for S3, Pericardial Rub)</li><li>Pulmonary Examination (Crackles, Pleural Effusion, Kussmaul Breathing)</li><li>Abdominal Examination (Inspection, Auscultation for Renal Bruits, Palpation of Kidneys, Percussion of Bladder, CVA Tenderness)</li><li>Genital Examination (Inspection of External Meatus, Scrotal Edema)</li><li>Rectal Examination (Prostate Assessment, Sphincter Tone)</li><li>Extremity Examination (Pitting Edema, AV Fistula Assessment, Gouty Tophi)</li><li>Fundoscopy</li><li>Lymph Node Examination</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"fourth-investigations-for-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Fourth: Investigations for the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>Blood Tests (Serum Creatinine, eGFR, BUN, Electrolytes, Calcium, Phosphate, Albumin, Serum Protein Electrophoresis, Serology)</li><li>Urine Studies (Dipstick Urinalysis, Urine Microscopy, Quantitative Proteinuria, Urine Osmolality and Electrolytes, Urine Culture)</li><li>Imaging (Renal Ultrasound with Doppler, CT Non-Contrast and CT Urography, Magnetic Resonance Angiography, Voiding Cystourethrogram, Nuclear Medicine Scans)</li><li>Renal Biopsy (Light Microscopy, Immunofluorescence, Electron Microscopy)</li><li>Genetic Testing</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"fifth-how-to-assess-the-system-clinically-and-ensure-it-is-healthy","عنوان":"Fifth: How to Assess the System Clinically and Ensure It Is Healthy","html":"<ul><li>History (Risk Factors for CKD, Family History, Medication Review)</li><li>Physical Examination (Normal Findings)</li><li>Screening Investigations (Blood Pressure, Serum Creatinine and eGFR, Urinalysis, Urine Albumin-to-Creatinine Ratio)</li><li>Assessment for CKD According to KDIGO 2024 Guidelines</li><li>Screening in Special Populations (Diabetes, Hypertension, Family History of Kidney Disease)</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"sixth-diseases-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Sixth: Diseases of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>Diabetic Nephropathy</li><li>Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis</li><li>Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)</li><li>Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN)</li><li>Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)</li><li>IgA Nephropathy (Berger&#39;s Disease)</li><li>Minimal Change Disease (MCD)</li><li>Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)</li><li>Membranous Nephropathy</li><li>Membranoproliferative Glomerulonephritis (MPGN) / C3 Glomerulopathy</li><li>Lupus Nephritis</li><li>Anti-GBM Disease (Goodpasture Syndrome)</li><li>ANCA-Associated Vasculitis (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis, Microscopic Polyangiitis, Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)</li><li>Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (HUS) and Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)</li><li>Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD)</li><li>Autosomal Recessive Polycystic Kidney Disease (ARPKD)</li><li>Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</li><li>Transitional Cell Carcinoma (Urothelial Carcinoma)</li><li>Urinary Tract Infection (Cystitis, Pyelonephritis)</li><li>Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)</li><li>Renal Artery Stenosis</li><li>Hepatorenal Syndrome</li><li>Cardiorenal Syndrome</li><li>Contrast-Induced Nephropathy</li><li>Rhabdomyolysis-Induced AKI</li><li>Myeloma Cast Nephropathy</li><li>HIV-Associated Nephropathy (HIVAN)</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"seventh-emergency-conditions-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Seventh: Emergency Conditions of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<ul><li>Life-Threatening Hyperkalemia</li><li>Rapidly Progressive Glomerulonephritis (RPGN)</li><li>Malignant Hypertension with Thrombotic Microangiopathy</li><li>Obstructive Uropathy with Acute Kidney Injury (Anuria)</li><li>Acute Pulmonary Edema from Volume Overload</li><li>Uremic Pericarditis with Tamponade</li><li>Hypertensive Encephalopathy (Posterior Reversible Encephalopathy Syndrome)</li><li>Calciphylaxis (Calcific Uremic Arteriolopathy)</li><li>Emphysematous Pyelonephritis</li><li>Spontaneous Renal Artery Dissection or Renal Vein Thrombosis</li><li>Acute Gout with Renal Impairment</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"eighth-pharmacological-drug-categories-in-nephrology","عنوان":"Eighth: Pharmacological Drug Categories in Nephrology","html":"<ul><li>Diuretics (Loop, Thiazide, Potassium-Sparing, Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors, Osmotic)</li><li>RAAS Inhibitors (ACE Inhibitors, ARBs, Direct Renin Inhibitors, ARNI, MRAs)</li><li>SGLT2 Inhibitors</li><li>GLP-1 Receptor Agonists</li><li>Vasopressin Receptor Antagonists (Vaptans)</li><li>Potassium Binders</li><li>Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents and HIF-PHIs</li><li>Phosphate Binders and Calcimimetics</li><li>Immunosuppressive Agents for Glomerular Disease</li><li>Antihypertensives (Beta-Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers, Direct Vasodilators)</li><li>Alkali Therapy</li><li>Analgesics for Renal Colic</li><li>Anticoagulants in Renal Impairment</li></ul><h3 id=\"comprehensive-nephrology-system\">Comprehensive Nephrology System</h3>"},{"معرّف":"first-symptoms-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"First: Symptoms of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<p>The kidneys and urinary tract produce symptoms that reflect disruption of their core functions: filtration, excretion, volume and electrolyte homeostasis, acid-base balance, hormone production, and urine transport. A complete, detailed list of every symptom originating from this system, with its definition and detailed pathogenic mechanism, follows.</p><h3 id=\"hematuria\">Hematuria</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> The presence of an abnormal number of red blood cells (erythrocytes) in the urine. It is defined quantitatively as ≥3-5 red blood cells per high-power field (HPF) on microscopic examination of a centrifuged urine sediment from a midstream, clean-catch specimen.¹ It is categorized as macroscopic (gross) hematuria, where blood is visible to the naked eye, changing the urine color to pink, red, or cola-colored, or microscopic hematuria, which is detected only by dipstick or microscopy.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Hematuria can originate from any point along the urinary tract, from the glomerulus to the urethral meatus. The mechanism and the morphology of the erythrocytes provide critical clues to the anatomical source. Hematuria is broadly divided into glomerular and non-glomerular (urological) causes.</p><ul><li><strong>Glomerular Hematuria:</strong> This results from structural or inflammatory disruption of the glomerular filtration barrier, the three-layered sieve comprising the fenestrated endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane (GBM), and the podocyte slit diaphragm. In proliferative glomerulonephritides (e.g., IgA nephropathy, post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, lupus nephritis, ANCA-associated vasculitis), immune complex deposition or direct antibody attack (anti-GBM disease) activates complement and attracts inflammatory cells (neutrophils, macrophages). These cells release proteolytic enzymes and reactive oxygen species that create focal breaks or rents in the GBM through which intact red blood cells can squeeze under the force of glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure. As erythrocytes traverse these narrow, irregular defects in the GBM and negotiate the narrow slit diaphragms, they are subjected to intense mechanical shear stress and osmotic stress. This causes them to emerge into the Bowman&#39;s space as <strong>dysmorphic red blood cells</strong>—cells that are misshapen, fragmented, and have irregular, bleb-like protrusions. The most pathognomonic form is the <strong>acanthocyte</strong> (also called a G1 cell), a ring-shaped erythrocyte with one or more vesicle-like protrusions, which is highly specific for glomerular bleeding.² The passage of dysmorphic RBCs through the tubules, where they become entrapped in the Tamm-Horsfall protein (uromodulin) gel secreted by the thick ascending limb cells, leads to the formation of <strong>red blood cell casts</strong>, which are cylindrical bodies that are virtually pathognomonic for glomerulonephritis. The urine in glomerular hematuria is often described as cola-colored, brown, or smoky, due to the formation of methemoglobin from hemoglobin in the acidic urine environment. Importantly, glomerular hematuria is typically painless unless there is associated renal capsular distension.</li><li><strong>Non-Glomerular (Urological) Hematuria:</strong> This arises from disruption of the urothelial lining of the renal calyces, pelvis, ureters, bladder, or urethra, or from the renal interstitium and tubules. In urinary tract infection, bacterial invasion (e.g., uropathogenic <em>Escherichia coli</em>) triggers an intense neutrophilic inflammatory response within the urothelium and submucosa. The resulting edema, hyperemia, and mucosal ulceration expose submucosal capillaries, which rupture, causing bleeding into the urine. In nephrolithiasis, a calculus migrating down the ureter causes direct mechanical abrasion and laceration of the urothelial surface, exposing blood vessels. In malignancy (transitional cell carcinoma or renal cell carcinoma), the abnormal, friable tumor vasculature (characterized by disordered angiogenesis, fragile capillary walls lacking normal smooth muscle support, and arteriovenous shunting) is prone to spontaneous rupture, even with minor trauma. In polycystic kidney disease, a cyst may rupture into the collecting system, or the massively enlarged, structurally abnormal kidneys are susceptible to minor trauma. In these non-glomerular causes, the red blood cells enter the urine after the glomerular filtration step; thus, they appear as <strong>isomorphic (normal, uniform) erythrocytes</strong> on microscopy, and <strong>red blood cell casts are absent</strong>. The bleeding may be associated with the passage of clots (which can be painful, causing ureteric colic from clot obstruction), and may be terminal (at the end of the urinary stream, suggesting a bladder neck or prostatic urethral source), initial (at the start of the stream, suggesting a distal urethral source), or total (throughout the stream, suggesting a source at the bladder or upper tracts).</li></ul><h3 id=\"proteinuria-frothy-urine\">Proteinuria / Frothy Urine</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> The excretion of an abnormal amount of protein in the urine. Normal daily urinary protein excretion is less than 150 mg per 24 hours, of which approximately 20 mg is albumin. Proteinuria is defined as excretion &gt;150 mg/day. It is clinically categorized as mild (150 mg to 1 g/day), moderate (1 g to 3.5 g/day), and nephrotic-range (&gt;3.5 g/day). Patients often report that their urine appears unusually &quot;frothy&quot; or &quot;foamy,&quot; producing bubbles that persist and do not flush away easily, analogous to the foam on a beer. This is due to the surfactant-like property of albumin, which lowers the surface tension of urine.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The excretion of protein in the urine is the net result of three interrelated processes: the amount filtered at the glomerulus, the amount reabsorbed by the proximal tubule, and the amount secreted by the distal tubule (Tamm-Horsfall protein).</p><ul><li><strong>Glomerular Proteinuria:</strong> This is the most common and clinically significant cause. The glomerular capillary wall is a highly selective filter that normally restricts the passage of macromolecules based on both size and charge. The normal filtration barrier restricts molecules with a molecular weight (MW) greater than 70,000 Daltons (like albumin, 66,500 Da) and restricts anionic molecules (albumin has a negative charge at physiological pH, pI ~4.7). In glomerular disease, this permselectivity is lost. In minimal change disease (MCD), the primary defect is podocyte injury by a circulating permeability factor (possibly angiopoietin-like-4 or hemopexin), which causes a universal <strong>effacement (flattening and retraction) of podocyte foot processes</strong>. The slit diaphragms between effaced foot processes become disorganized; the key protein <strong>nephrin</strong> is internalized and degraded. The loss of the slit diaphragm&#39;s size-selective function creates large, non-selective &quot;pores&quot; through which albumin can freely pass into the Bowman&#39;s space. In membranous nephropathy, autoantibodies (most commonly against the phospholipase A2 receptor, PLA2R, on the podocyte cell surface) form subepithelial immune deposits. These deposits activate complement, leading to the insertion of the C5b-9 membrane attack complex (MAC) into the podocyte membrane. Rather than causing cell lysis, sublytic MAC insertion activates podocytes, causing them to produce excessive extracellular matrix, and disrupts the slit diaphragm structure, causing a loss of the charge barrier. In focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS), a circulating factor (such as soluble urokinase plasminogen activator receptor, suPAR) activates podocyte β3 integrins, causing podocyte contraction, detachment from the GBM, and cell death (podocytopenia). The denuded GBM areas provide a direct, unrestricted pathway for protein filtration, leading to massive proteinuria.³</li><li><strong>Tubular Proteinuria:</strong> Normally, the small amount of low-molecular-weight proteins (MW &lt;25,000 Da, such as β2-microglobulin, α1-microglobulin, retinol-binding protein, and lysozyme) that are freely filtered by the glomerulus are almost completely reabsorbed by the proximal tubular epithelial cells via receptor-mediated endocytosis (mediated by the tandem receptors megalin and cubilin). In diseases that damage the proximal tubular epithelium (acute tubular necrosis, acute interstitial nephritis, Fanconi syndrome, heavy metal toxicity, or damage from myeloma light chains), this reabsorptive capacity is impaired. This results in increased urinary excretion of these low-molecular-weight proteins. The total amount of proteinuria is typically mild (&lt;1-2 g/day), and the urine dipstick, which is specific for albumin, may be negative or trace, as the lost proteins are not albumin.</li><li><strong>Overflow Proteinuria:</strong> This occurs when the plasma concentration of a filterable, low-molecular-weight protein increases so dramatically that the filtered load far exceeds the normal, fixed maximum reabsorptive capacity (Tm) of the proximal tubule. The classic example is multiple myeloma, where the malignant plasma cells produce a monoclonal excess of immunoglobulin light chains (kappa or lambda). These light chains, known as Bence-Jones proteins, are small enough to be freely filtered. When their filtered load exceeds the reabsorptive maximum, they spill into the urine. In hemolytic anemias with massive intravascular hemolysis, hemoglobin is released and filtered, exceeding the tubular reabsorption of hemoglobin-haptoglobin complexes. In rhabdomyolysis, myoglobin from damaged muscle is filtered, leading to a positive urine dipstick for &quot;blood&quot; (which detects the heme moiety) despite the absence of red blood cells on microscopy.</li></ul><h3 id=\"edema\">Edema</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Edema is the clinically detectable accumulation of excess interstitial fluid, manifesting as a palpable swelling. In renal disease, it has a predilection for loose, distensible tissues. It first appears in the periorbital region, particularly noticeable upon waking in the morning, because the low-tissue-pressure eyelid skin allows fluid to accumulate during recumbency. As the condition progresses, it descends and manifests as pitting edema of the lower extremities (ankles and pretibial area), and in severe cases, fluid accumulates in the pleural cavity (hydrothorax), peritoneal cavity (ascites), and throughout the subcutaneous tissue (anasarca). Fluid weight gain of 5-10 kg may occur before edema is clinically obvious.⁴</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Renal edema can be caused by two primary, distinct, and sometimes overlapping mechanisms: the &quot;underfill&quot; mechanism of nephrotic syndrome and the &quot;overfill&quot; mechanism of nephritic syndrome and acute kidney injury.</p><ul><li><strong>Edema in Nephrotic Syndrome (Underfill Hypothesis):</strong> The initiating event is the massive urinary loss of albumin, which decreases the plasma oncotic pressure (colloid osmotic pressure, normally ~25-28 mmHg). According to the Starling equilibrium governing fluid exchange across capillary walls, the reduction in the force that retains fluid within the intravascular space leads to a net shift of fluid from the plasma into the interstitial space. This translocation of fluid reduces the effective circulating volume (ECV). The reduction in ECV is sensed by the juxtaglomerular apparatus in the afferent arteriole and the baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch as true hypovolemia. This triggers a cascade of neurohumoral compensatory responses that are teleologically designed to restore volume but pathologically worsen edema: activation of the sympathetic nervous system, stimulation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), and non-osmotic release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Aldosterone stimulates intense sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct; ADH promotes water reabsorption. The kidney, perceiving a volume deficit, avidly retains the ingested sodium and water, but because the primary problem is low oncotic pressure, the retained fluid simply leaks back out into the interstitium, perpetuating a vicious cycle of progressive edema formation. However, the &quot;overfill&quot; hypothesis posits that in many patients with nephrotic syndrome, the primary defect is an intrinsic, intra-renal sodium retention, perhaps mediated by an abnormal, constitutively active epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting duct, as evidenced by the fact that many nephrotic patients are volume-expanded and have suppressed renin and aldosterone levels.⁵</li><li><strong>Edema in Acute Kidney Injury and Nephritic Syndrome (Overfill Mechanism):</strong> In these conditions, the primary event is a reduction in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). The kidney&#39;s capacity to filter and excrete sodium and water is severely compromised. The patient continues to ingest sodium and water, resulting in a progressive, positive external balance. This leads to an expansion of the extracellular fluid volume, including the plasma volume. The kidneys are intrinsically diseased and cannot mount a natriuretic response. The hydrostatic pressure in the capillaries rises, forcing fluid into the interstitium. This is a state of true, primary sodium and water excess, and the edema occurs in the setting of normal or even elevated plasma oncotic pressure.</li></ul><h3 id=\"oliguria-and-anuria\">Oliguria and Anuria</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Oliguria is a reduction in urine output. It is defined in adults as a urine output of less than 400-500 mL per 24 hours, or less than 0.5 mL per kilogram of body weight per hour. This represents the minimum volume of urine in which the kidneys can excrete the obligate daily solute load (~600 mOsm/day) when the urine is maximally concentrated (1200 mOsm/L). Anuria is the near-complete or complete absence of urine production, defined as less than 100 mL per 24 hours. It is a medical emergency.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The causes are traditionally classified as pre-renal, intrinsic renal, and post-renal.</p><ul><li><strong>Pre-renal Oliguria:</strong> This is a functional, reversible response to renal hypoperfusion. The kidneys are structurally normal, but they receive inadequate blood flow due to true volume depletion (hemorrhage, vomiting, diarrhea, diuretic use) or a reduction in effective circulating volume (heart failure, cirrhosis). The baroreceptor-mediated neurohumoral response (sympathetic activation, RAAS, ADH) causes maximal renal sodium and water avidity. The kidney produces a small volume of highly concentrated urine (urine osmolality &gt;500 mOsm/L) with a very low sodium content (urine Na⁺ &lt;20 mEq/L, FENa &lt;1%). If the hypoperfusion is severe and prolonged, it transitions to intrinsic ATN.</li><li><strong>Intrinsic Renal Oliguria:</strong> This results from structural damage to the renal parenchyma. In acute tubular necrosis (ATN), ischemia or nephrotoxins cause death and sloughing of tubular epithelial cells. The cellular debris and cast material obstruct the tubular lumen, preventing the flow of filtrate. Simultaneously, there is afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction (reducing GFR) and back-leak of glomerular filtrate across the denuded, damaged tubular basement membrane into the interstitium and back into the circulation. In severe, crescentic glomerulonephritis, the Bowman&#39;s space is obliterated by proliferating cells and fibrin (crescents), blocking filtration at the level of the glomerulus. In acute interstitial nephritis, the intense interstitial edema and inflammatory infiltrate compress the tubules, obstructing flow.</li><li><strong>Post-Renal Oliguria/Anuria:</strong> This occurs when there is an obstruction to the flow of urine from both kidneys, or from a single functioning kidney, or when there is complete bladder outlet obstruction. The obstruction causes a rise in hydrostatic pressure in the renal collecting system, which is transmitted retrogradely through the nephron to Bowman&#39;s space. When the pressure in Bowman&#39;s space (P_BS) rises to equal the glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (P_GC), the net filtration pressure becomes zero, and glomerular filtration ceases entirely. Anuria is the hallmark symptom of complete bilateral ureteric obstruction (e.g., from retroperitoneal fibrosis encasing both ureters, or a pelvic malignancy) or a complete bladder outlet obstruction (e.g., a blocked Foley catheter in a patient with a neurogenic bladder).⁶</li></ul><h3 id=\"polyuria\">Polyuria</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Polyuria is the production of an abnormally large volume of urine. It is typically defined as a 24-hour urine output exceeding 3 liters in adults. The patient complains of passing large volumes of urine frequently, day and night.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Polyuria is always the result of either a water diuresis (excretion of large volumes of dilute urine, urine osmolality &lt;250 mOsm/L) or a solute (osmotic) diuresis (excretion of large volumes of iso-osmotic or slightly hyperosmotic urine, urine osmolality &gt;300 mOsm/L).</p><ul><li><strong>Water Diuresis (Impaired Urinary Concentration):</strong> The kidney&#39;s ability to concentrate urine depends on the generation of a hypertonic medullary interstitium (via the countercurrent multiplier) and the presence of ADH, which makes the collecting duct permeable to water. A water diuresis occurs when there is a deficiency of ADH (central diabetes insipidus, due to destruction of the hypothalamic nuclei) or resistance of the collecting duct to its action (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus). In nephrogenic DI, the ADH V2 receptor may be defective (congenital X-linked form), the aquaporin-2 water channel may be mutated (congenital autosomal recessive form), or the countercurrent mechanism may be disrupted (as in chronic lithium therapy, which downregulates AQP2 expression; hypercalcemia; hypokalemia; or chronic kidney disease). In the absence of ADH action, the collecting duct remains impermeable to water. The dilute fluid (100 mOsm/L) entering from the TAL remains dilute, and as Na⁺ is further reabsorbed in the DCT and collecting duct, the urine becomes even more dilute (as low as 50 mOsm/L), resulting in a high-volume, dilute urine output. Primary polydipsia (psychogenic or dipsogenic) leads to a massive increase in water intake, which expands body water, slightly lowers plasma osmolality, and physiologically suppresses ADH release, causing a compensatory, appropriate water diuresis.</li><li><strong>Solute (Osmotic) Diuresis:</strong> This occurs when the tubular fluid contains a high concentration of an osmotically active solute that is not efficiently reabsorbed by the nephron. This solute exerts an osmotic force that obligates water to stay with it in the tubular lumen, overwhelming the reabsorptive capacity of the collecting duct. The classic example is the glucosuria of uncontrolled diabetes mellitus: when plasma glucose far exceeds the renal threshold (~200 mg/dL), the filtered load of glucose exceeds the maximum reabsorptive capacity (Tm) of the proximal tubular SGLT2 and SGLT1 transporters. The non-reabsorbed glucose in the tubular lumen acts as an osmotic diuretic, dragging water, sodium, and potassium with it and causing severe polyuria and volume depletion. Other causes include mannitol infusion, urea diuresis (following the relief of urinary tract obstruction or after a high-protein tube feed), and the therapeutic use of SGLT2 inhibitors.</li></ul><h3 id=\"nocturia\">Nocturia</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Nocturia is defined by the International Continence Society as the complaint that the individual has to wake at night one or more times to void. It is a highly prevalent and bothersome symptom, profoundly disturbing sleep architecture and quality of life. It is clinically significant when the patient voids two or more times per night.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Nocturia in renal patients is multifactorial.</p><ul><li><strong>Nocturnal Polyuria (Increased Nocturnal Urine Production):</strong> In chronic kidney disease, the progressive loss of functioning nephrons leads to the loss of the hypertonic medullary interstitium. The kidney loses its ability to concentrate urine maximally (isosthenuria, fixed specific gravity of 1.010). To excrete the day&#39;s obligate solute load, the kidney must produce a larger volume of less concentrated urine. This solute diuresis continues throughout the night, producing a nocturnal urine volume that exceeds bladder capacity. In congestive heart failure, dependent peripheral edema accumulates during the day due to gravity. When the patient assumes the supine position at night, the hydrostatic pressure in the lower extremities decreases, and this sequestered interstitial fluid is mobilized back into the intravascular compartment. This increases renal blood flow, GFR, and urine output (&quot;reverse diurnal pattern&quot;). In obstructive sleep apnea, the negative intrathoracic pressure generated during apneic episodes increases venous return and stretches the atrial myocardium, causing a surge in ANP release, which promotes natriuresis and water diuresis.</li><li><strong>Reduced Bladder Capacity:</strong> In men with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) and bladder outlet obstruction, the detrusor muscle hypertrophies to overcome the obstruction. This reduces bladder compliance. The bladder holds less urine before the intravesical pressure triggers the micturition reflex, and the sensation of fullness and urgency occurs at lower volumes. A similar mechanism occurs in any condition causing detrusor overactivity.</li><li><strong>Global Polyuria:</strong> If a patient has any cause of 24-hour polyuria (e.g., poorly controlled diabetes mellitus, diabetes insipidus, excessive fluid intake), the high daytime urine output continues into the night, causing nocturia.</li></ul><h3 id=\"dysuria\">Dysuria</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Dysuria is the sensation of pain, discomfort, or burning during or immediately after urination. It is the hallmark symptom of lower urinary tract inflammation. The pain is typically localized to the distal urethra in women (internal dysuria) or along the penile urethra in men (external dysuria).</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The inflamed, edematous, and hyperemic mucosa of the bladder (cystitis) or urethra (urethritis) is exquisitely sensitive. During voiding, the detrusor muscle contracts, and the urine stream flows over and distends the inflamed mucosal surface. The exposure of the bare, damaged submucosal nerve endings (nociceptors) to the acidic, hyperosmolar urine, and to inflammatory mediators (prostaglandins E2 and I2, bradykinin, substance P, and histamine), which are released by infiltrating leukocytes and damaged urothelial cells, directly activates and sensitizes these pain fibers. The burning pain is produced as the urine passes. The pain is most intense at the end of micturition (strangury), as the bladder walls rub together. In acute cystitis, the inflammation is confined to the bladder mucosa, and dysuria is often accompanied by frequency and urgency. In urethritis, the inflammation is localized to the urethra, and the dysuria may be felt at the start of micturition.</p><h3 id=\"urinary-frequency-and-urgency\">Urinary Frequency and Urgency</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Urinary frequency is the need to void more often than is normal for the patient, typically more than 8 times during waking hours. Urgency is a sudden, compelling, and difficult-to-defer desire to pass urine. When urgency is accompanied by the fear of leakage, it is termed urge incontinence.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The normal micturition reflex is a complex, coordinated process governed by the pontine micturition center. During the filling phase, the bladder detrusor muscle is relaxed (mediated by sympathetic beta-3 receptor activation), and the urethral sphincter is contracted. In cystitis, the bacterial endotoxins and the host inflammatory response cause mast cell degranulation, release of histamine and cytokines, and damage to the protective glycosaminoglycan layer of the urothelium. This exposes the underlying sensory C-fiber afferent nerves to the urine contents. These C-fibers become hypersensitive and fire at abnormally low bladder volumes, generating a premature, intense sensation of bladder fullness (frequency) and triggering a detrusor contraction (urgency) before the bladder is full. In neurogenic detrusor overactivity (from spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, or stroke), there is a loss of the descending inhibitory input from the cerebral cortex and pontine micturition center to the sacral micturition center. This results in an uninhibited, hyperreflexic detrusor contraction in response to minimal bladder distension.</p><h3 id=\"flank-pain-and-loin-pain\">Flank Pain and Loin Pain</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Pain localized to the flank, the area between the lower ribs and the iliac crest, often radiating anteriorly and inferiorly toward the groin. It can range from a dull, constant ache to an excruciating, paroxysmal, colicky pain.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Two primary mechanisms generate flank pain: capsular distension and ureteral obstruction.</p><ul><li><strong>Renal Capsular Distension:</strong> The renal capsule is a fibrous sac that surrounds the kidney. It is innervated by pain fibers from the sympathetic plexus (T10-L1). When the renal parenchyma expands acutely, it stretches the capsule, causing a dull, constant, aching pain in the costovertebral angle (CVA) that is not relieved by changes in position. This is the pain of acute pyelonephritis (where intense neutrophilic infiltration and interstitial edema cause the kidney to swell), of acute glomerulonephritis, or of a rapidly growing renal cell carcinoma. In autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD), acute cyst hemorrhage causes rapid cyst expansion, stretching the capsule.</li><li><strong>Ureteral Colic:</strong> This is the severe, paroxysmal pain of ureteral obstruction, most commonly from a calculus. The obstruction causes an acute rise in intraluminal pressure proximal to the stone. This triggers the release of prostaglandins (especially PGE2), which cause intense, uncoordinated, hyperperistaltic contractions of the ureteral smooth muscle as it attempts to force the stone distally. This spasm, combined with the pressure-induced stretching of the nerve endings in the ureteral wall, produces the characteristic colicky pain—waves of severe, cramping pain that radiate from the flank to the ipsilateral lower quadrant of the abdomen, and into the testicle or labium (because the ureter and the gonads share the same sensory nerve supply, T11-L2). The patient is unable to find a comfortable position and writhes in agony, in stark contrast to the patient with peritonitis who lies perfectly still.</li></ul><h3 id=\"uremic-symptoms-constitutional\">Uremic Symptoms (Constitutional)</h3><p>These are systemic symptoms caused by the toxic accumulation of nitrogenous and other metabolic waste products in advanced, untreated renal failure (usually GFR &lt;15-20 mL/min).</p><ul><li><strong>Fatigue and Weakness:</strong> This is the most common, earliest, and most disabling symptom of advancing uremia. It is profoundly multifactorial. The normocytic, normochromic anemia of CKD, caused by the insufficient production of erythropoietin by the diseased renal peritubular fibroblasts, reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, leading to tissue hypoxia and easy fatiguability. The accumulation of uremic toxins (urea, indoxyl sulfate, p-cresyl sulfate, guanidino compounds) directly impairs mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation and ATP generation in skeletal muscle cells. Metabolic acidosis promotes muscle protein catabolism and wasting (protein-energy wasting, PEW), leading to loss of lean body mass and physical weakness. The chronic, low-grade systemic inflammation of uremia (driven by cytokine release, oxidative stress, and gut permeability to bacterial endotoxins) causes anorexia and malaise.</li><li><strong>Anorexia, Nausea, and Vomiting:</strong> Uremic toxins stimulate the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the area postrema of the medulla oblongata, a circumventricular organ that lies outside the blood-brain barrier and directly senses the biochemical derangement of the plasma. The accumulation of gut-derived uremic toxins (products of bacterial fermentation of amino acids in the colon) causes a chemical gastroenteritis, directly irritating the GI mucosa and altering motility. Anorexia is a cardinal feature, and its progressive worsening is a key clinical marker for the need to initiate dialysis.</li><li><strong>Pruritus (Uremic Pruritus):</strong> A persistent, intense, and often disabling generalized itching. The skin is typically dry and excoriated from scratching, but often without a primary rash. The pathogenesis is complex and incompletely understood but involves: (1) deposition of calcium phosphate microcrystals in the skin due to an elevated calcium-phosphate product in secondary hyperparathyroidism; (2) elevated levels of parathyroid hormone (PTH), which can directly stimulate mast cell degranulation and histamine release; (3) xerosis (severe dry skin) from atrophy of sebaceous and sweat glands; (4) accumulation of uremic toxins that directly stimulate the opioid mu-receptors and kappa-receptors in the skin nerve endings; and (5) peripheral neuropathy with sensitization of unmyelinated C-fibers.</li><li><strong>Sleep Disturbance and Restless Legs Syndrome:</strong> Uremic patients suffer from severe insomnia, daytime somnolence, and a reversal of the sleep-wake cycle. Restless legs syndrome is a distressing, irresistible urge to move the legs, associated with uncomfortable crawling, creeping, or aching sensations deep in the limbs, occurring at rest and worse at night. The pathogenesis is linked to central dopamine dysregulation in the basal ganglia and impaired dopaminergic transmission, as well as iron deficiency affecting the substantia nigra.</li><li><strong>Dysgeusia (Metallic Taste, Ammoniacal Taste):</strong> A persistent, unpleasant metallic or bitter taste in the mouth. It results from the elevated concentration of urea in the saliva. Oral bacteria express the enzyme urease, which breaks down salivary urea into ammonia (NH₃) and carbon dioxide. The volatile ammonia gas imparts the characteristic fetid, ammoniacal odor to the breath (uremic fetor), and the alkaline ammonia irritates the taste buds, causing dysgeusia and reducing food palatability.</li></ul><h3 id=\"urinary-hesitancy-poor-stream-intermittency-and-terminal-dribbling\">Urinary Hesitancy, Poor Stream, Intermittency, and Terminal Dribbling</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> These are the obstructive lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) classically associated with bladder outlet obstruction. <strong>Hesitancy</strong> is difficulty initiating the urinary stream, with the patient straining to start voiding. <strong>Poor (weak) stream</strong> is a loss of the normal force and caliber of the urine stream. <strong>Intermittency</strong> is a stream that starts and stops several times during one void. <strong>Terminal dribbling</strong> is the slow, continued leakage of a small amount of urine at the end of voiding, after the main stream has ceased.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common cause is benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). The transition zone of the prostate, which surrounds the prostatic urethra, undergoes nodular hyperplasia under the influence of dihydrotestosterone (DHT). The enlarged prostatic tissue physically compresses and narrows the prostatic urethra, increasing urethral resistance. To overcome this obstruction, the detrusor muscle must contract more forcefully, leading to detrusor hypertrophy. However, this eventually becomes insufficient to fully empty the bladder. The obstructed, turbulent flow explains the poor stream and intermittency. The post-void residual volume in the bladder increases. The detrusor muscle, chronically overstretched and weakened, cannot sustain a contraction to the end of voiding, causing terminal dribbling.</p><h3 id=\"pneumaturia-and-fecaluria\">Pneumaturia and Fecaluria</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Pneumaturia is the passage of gas (air bubbles) during urination. Fecaluria is the passage of fecal matter in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Both are pathognomonic for an enterovesical fistula—an abnormal, fistulous connection between the bowel lumen and the bladder lumen. The most common cause is diverticulitis (especially sigmoid diverticulitis), where a colonic diverticulum ruptures and erodes into the adjacent bladder wall. Other causes include Crohn&#39;s disease (transmural inflammation creating a fistula), colorectal or bladder cancer, and radiation therapy. Gas produced by colonic bacteria and fecal material enters the bladder and is passed during micturition. These patients typically present with recurrent, polymicrobial urinary tract infections.</p><h3 id=\"urethral-discharge\">Urethral Discharge</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> An abnormal, visible discharge from the urethral meatus, occurring independently of urination.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> It is the hallmark symptom of urethritis, a sexually transmitted infection. In gonococcal urethritis (caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>), the bacteria attach to and invade the columnar epithelial cells of the urethra, eliciting a massive, purulent neutrophilic inflammatory exudate. The discharge is typically thick, copious, and frankly purulent (yellow or greenish). In non-gonococcal urethritis (caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, <em>Mycoplasma genitalium</em>, or <em>Ureaplasma urealyticum</em>), the discharge is usually scantier, thinner, and may be clear, white, or mucoid (&quot;morning drop&quot;).</p>"},{"معرّف":"second-signs-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Second: Signs of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<p>A complete, exhaustive list of all clinical signs originating from the renal system, with their definitions and detailed pathogenic mechanisms.</p><h3 id=\"hypertension\">Hypertension</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A sustained elevation of systemic arterial blood pressure. According to the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline, hypertension is defined as a systolic blood pressure (SBP) ≥130 mmHg or a diastolic blood pressure (DBP) ≥80 mmHg. KDIGO endorses a target BP of &lt;120 systolic for non-dialysis CKD, based on the SPRINT trial.⁷</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis in Renal Disease:</strong> Hypertension and kidney disease are inextricably linked in a bidirectional relationship. The kidney is both a victim and a perpetrator of hypertension. The pathogenesis is multifactorial.</p><ul><li><strong>Sodium and Volume Retention (Volume-Dependent Hypertension):</strong> The most fundamental mechanism. When GFR declines, the kidney&#39;s capacity to excrete a given sodium load is reduced. Sodium accumulates in the extracellular fluid, and because sodium is an impermeant extracellular solute, water is retained osmotically. The expansion of ECF volume raises cardiac output and total peripheral resistance via whole-body autoregulation. This type of hypertension is characterized by a suppressed plasma renin activity and is most responsive to diuretics and sodium restriction. In ESRD, this is the dominant mechanism, and blood pressure is exquisitely dependent on the control of extracellular volume through ultrafiltration on dialysis.</li><li><strong>Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) Activation (Renin-Dependent Hypertension):</strong> In diseases causing renal ischemia, such as renal artery stenosis (atherosclerotic or fibromuscular dysplasia), the reduced perfusion pressure in the afferent arteriole distal to the stenosis is sensed by the JGA as a baroreceptor signal, causing a massive, inappropriate release of renin. Renin drives a supraphysiological production of angiotensin II (a direct, powerful vasoconstrictor) and aldosterone (which drives sodium retention). This creates a high-renin, angiotensin II-driven hypertension.</li><li><strong>Sympathetic Nervous System Overactivity:</strong> In CKD, there is a state of heightened efferent sympathetic nerve traffic, documented by microneurography. The mechanisms include afferent signals from the diseased, scarred kidney to the brain (re-renal reflex) and impaired baroreflex sensitivity. This increases vascular tone and renin release.</li><li><strong>Endothelial Dysfunction and Increased Vascular Resistance:</strong> CKD is a state of oxidative stress and chronic inflammation. The accumulation of asymmetric dimethylarginine (ADMA), an endogenous inhibitor of endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS), reduces nitric oxide production, impairing endothelium-dependent vasodilation. The production of the potent vasoconstrictor endothelin-1 is increased. These lead to a state of fixed, increased systemic vascular resistance.</li><li><strong>Arterial Stiffness:</strong> Vascular calcification, which is actively promoted in uremia by elevated phosphate, calcium, FGF23, and the deficiency of calcification inhibitors (fetuin-A, matrix Gla protein), causes a loss of the elastic properties of the aorta. This increases pulse wave velocity and systolic blood pressure, and widens the pulse pressure.</li></ul><h3 id=\"pallor\">Pallor</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> An abnormal paleness of the skin and the mucous membranes (conjunctivae, lips, nail beds). In renal patients, it is typically a sallow, &quot;dirty&quot;-looking pallor, rather than the pure, bright-white pallor of acute blood loss.</p><h3 id=\"pathogenesis\">Pathogenesis:</h3><ul><li><strong>Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease:</strong> This is the primary mechanism. The renal peritubular fibroblasts are the body&#39;s main source of the glycoprotein hormone erythropoietin (EPO). EPO production is tightly regulated by the oxygen-sensing HIF-prolyl hydroxylase pathway. In CKD, the progressive fibrosis and destruction of the renal interstitium damages these EPO-producing cells, leading to an absolute or relative EPO deficiency. This results in a hypoproliferative, normocytic, normochromic anemia, where the bone marrow cannot produce red blood cells at a rate sufficient to replace the normal daily turnover. A low reticulocyte index is a key feature. The dilutional effect of an expanded plasma volume due to sodium and water retention also contributes to a reduced hemoglobin concentration.</li><li><strong>Inhibitors of Erythropoiesis:</strong> Uremic toxins and the chronic inflammatory state (elevated IL-1, IL-6, TNF-α) inhibit erythropoiesis directly at the level of the bone marrow CFU-E and indirectly by increasing the liver production of hepcidin, a peptide hormone that blocks iron absorption from the duodenum and traps iron in the reticuloendothelial system (macrophages), causing a functional iron deficiency (iron-restricted erythropoiesis) even when total body iron stores are adequate.</li></ul><h3 id=\"hyperpigmentation-uremic-pigmentation\">Hyperpigmentation (Uremic Pigmentation)</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A characteristic, diffuse, brownish-yellow or sallow discoloration of the skin, most evident on sun-exposed areas and the face. It is distinct from jaundice (which has a yellow-greenish, bilirubin-induced hue).</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The primary mechanism is the progressive, systemic accumulation of retained urinary pigments and chromogens that are normally excreted by the kidney. These include urochromes (the yellow pigments that give urine its normal color) and carotenoids. These lipid-soluble, pigmented metabolites are deposited in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue. Melanocyte-stimulating hormone (β-lipotropin) levels are increased in renal failure, which may stimulate increased melanin production, contributing to the brownish component.</p><h3 id=\"asterixis\">Asterixis</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A neurological sign of metabolic encephalopathy, most commonly hepatic but also uremic. It is an involuntary, intermittent loss of sustained posture, described as a &quot;flapping tremor.&quot; To elicit it, the examiner asks the patient to extend their arms, dorsiflex their wrists, and spread their fingers. A positive test is the observation of sudden, brief, non-rhythmic flexion movements of the wrists and fingers, resembling a bird flapping its wings, occurring after a latent period of a few seconds.⁸</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Asterixis is a negative myoclonus. It is due to a transient, intermittent loss of muscle tone in the extensor muscles of the wrist and hand, maintained by the postural system of the brainstem reticular formation. The accumulation of uremic toxins (especially guanidino compounds, phenols, and middle molecules) disrupts the normal function of the reticular activating system and the basal ganglia-thalamocortical circuits that regulate motor tone. Uremia also impairs the brain&#39;s ability to detoxify ammonia and synthesize neurotransmitters, contributing to this phenomenon of metabolic encephalopathy.</p><h3 id=\"pericardial-friction-rub\">Pericardial Friction Rub</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A characteristic, highly specific auscultatory sign of pericardial inflammation. It is a grating, scratching, to-and-fro sound, often described as having a &quot;leather-on-leather&quot; or &quot;Velcro&quot; quality. It is typically heard best over the left lower sternal border, with the diaphragm of the stethoscope, and with the patient sitting up, leaning forward, and holding their breath in full expiration. It is classically triphasic (corresponding to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and ventricular diastole), but is often only biphasic or monophasic.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Uremic pericarditis is a sterile, fibrinous, hemorrhagic inflammation of the visceral and parietal pericardial surfaces. It was historically a preterminal event in advanced uremia. The exact cause is unknown, but it is attributed to the direct chemical irritation of the serosal surfaces by uremic toxins (particularly the nitrogenous waste products) that are excreted across the pericardial mesothelium. The inflamed, roughened surfaces, coated with a shaggy, fibrin-rich exudate, rub against each other with each contraction and relaxation of the heart. Because the inflammation is fibrin-rich rather than producing a massive effusion, the rub can be heard even when a large effusion is present (unlike other forms of pericarditis). It is an absolute indication for the urgent initiation or intensification of dialysis.⁹</p><h3 id=\"uremic-frost\">Uremic Frost</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A rare, virtually pathognomonic, physical sign of extreme, preterminal azotemia. It appears as a fine, white, or yellowish-white crystalline powder deposited on the skin, most prominently on the face (around the nares, eyebrows, and beard area), neck, and trunk. It is now exceedingly rare in developed countries due to the universal availability of dialysis.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> As the blood urea nitrogen (BUN) level climbs to extraordinarily high levels, typically exceeding 250-300 mg/dL, the concentration of urea in the extracellular fluid reaches a point where it is secreted in high concentrations by the eccrine sweat glands. The water component of the sweat rapidly evaporates from the warm skin surface, leaving behind a visible, crystalline residue of concentrated urea. This finding is a clear indicator of profound renal failure and an immediate need for dialysis.</p><h3 id=\"pitting-edema-and-ascites\">Pitting Edema and Ascites</h3><p><strong>Definition of Pitting Edema:</strong> A clinically detectable excess of interstitial fluid in the subcutaneous tissues. It is elicited by applying firm, steady pressure with the thumb or index finger for at least 5 seconds over a bony prominence—most commonly the distal third of the tibia or the sacrum (in a bed-bound patient). If pitting is present, the finger will leave a persistent, visible indentation or &quot;pit&quot; in the skin that slowly refills, indicating that the fluid has been displaced and is slowly flowing back. The depth and duration of the pit are semi-quantitatively graded from 1+ (barely detectable) to 4+ (a deep pit that persists for minutes).</p><p><strong>Definition of Ascites:</strong> The pathological accumulation of fluid within the peritoneal cavity.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The edema of renal disease is detailed in the Symptoms section. The physical sign is the palpable and visible result of the pathophysiology of sodium and water retention. Ascites forms when the hepatic sinusoids are congested from right ventricular failure secondary to volume overload, or, in nephrotic syndrome, when the hypoalbuminemia and positive sodium balance lead to transudation across the peritoneal mesothelium. Ascites is detected by the physical signs of flank bulging, shifting dullness, and a transmitted fluid wave (fluid thrill).</p><h3 id=\"palpable-kidney\">Palpable Kidney</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> The ability to palpate a kidney on deep bimanual abdominal palpation, using a ballottement technique. In normal adults, the kidneys are usually not palpable, except for the lower pole of the right kidney in a very thin individual.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis and Significance:</strong> A palpable kidney is an abnormal finding that indicates that the kidney is enlarged.</p><ul><li><strong>Bilateral, massively enlarged, and irregularly bosselated (nodular) kidneys</strong> are the hallmark of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). The kidneys are replaced by thousands of fluid-filled cysts of varying sizes, which can expand the kidney to the size of a football and make it easily palpable in the flank and across the midline. The irregular, nodular contour is the defining physical sign.</li><li><strong>A unilateral, smooth, firm, non-tender enlargement</strong> is concerning for a renal tumor (renal cell carcinoma) or severe hydronephrosis. In hydronephrosis, the kidney becomes a tense, fluid-filled sac.</li><li><strong>Ballottement technique:</strong> The examiner&#39;s left hand is placed behind the patient&#39;s flank, and the fingers of the right hand are pressed firmly and deeply into the anterior abdominal wall. The posterior hand flicks the kidney forward, and if enlarged, it can be felt to bounce against the anterior hand (&quot;bump&quot;).</li></ul><h3 id=\"distended-bladder\">Distended Bladder</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A visible or palpable, tense, rounded, dome-shaped mass arising from the pelvis in the suprapubic region. It is dull to percussion. It represents a chronically or acutely overfilled bladder due to urinary retention.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common cause is bladder outlet obstruction, most often from benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). As the obstruction progresses, the detrusor muscle, which initially compensates by hypertrophying, eventually fails and decompensates. The post-void residual urine volume increases with each void, gradually distending the bladder. In acute urinary retention, often precipitated by an anticholinergic drug, an alpha-agonist (cold medication), or alcohol, the patient experiences sudden inability to void despite an agonizing urge, and a massively distended bladder is palpable suprapubically. In neurogenic bladder (e.g., from diabetic autonomic neuropathy or spinal cord injury), the patient loses the sensation of a full bladder, and the bladder silently distends, holding over 1-2 liters of urine (overflow incontinence). The bladder is dull to percussion because it is a fluid-filled organ.</p><h3 id=\"costovertebral-angle-cva-tenderness\">Costovertebral Angle (CVA) Tenderness</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Pain elicited by percussion over the costovertebral angle, which is the angle formed by the junction of the inferior border of the 12th rib and the lateral border of the paraspinal muscles of the vertebral column.</p><p><strong>Technique:</strong> The examiner places the palm of one hand flat over the patient&#39;s CVA on the back. The examiner then uses the ulnar aspect of the closed fist of the other hand to deliver a firm, controlled thump onto the dorsum of the hand resting on the patient. The patient should not be warned of the exact timing. A positive sign is the patient experiencing sharp, jolting pain, often causing them to wince or jerk away.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The blow transmits a shock wave through the soft tissues to the kidney. If the kidney is acutely inflamed and swollen (acute pyelonephritis), the renal capsule is already tense and stretched. The percussion causes a rapid, painful distension of the capsule, activating its sensory nerve fibers (T10-L1). Similarly, acute obstruction of the kidney causes capsular distension, and the percussion exacerbates it. Bilateral CVA tenderness suggests a systemic inflammatory process like acute glomerulonephritis. The sign has good sensitivity (around 85%) for acute pyelonephritis, but its specificity is limited because musculoskeletal back pain or rib pathology can also cause discomfort.</p><h3 id=\"pulmonary-crackles-and-elevated-jvp\">Pulmonary Crackles and Elevated JVP</h3><p><strong>Pulmonary Crackles (Rales):</strong> A discontinuous, fine, high-pitched, end-inspiratory crackling or popping sound heard on auscultation of the lung bases. It does not clear with coughing. It is the hallmark auscultatory finding of pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Elevated JVP:</strong> A sustained elevation of the jugular venous pressure, measured as the vertical height of the top of the pulsating jugular venous column above the sternal angle of Louis. A height &gt;3 cm is abnormal and indicates elevated central venous pressure.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Both signs are the direct consequences of the failure of the diseased kidneys to excrete sodium and water, leading to an expansion of the total plasma volume. This increases the left ventricular filling pressure. When the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure exceeds the plasma oncotic pressure (~25 mmHg), fluid transudes from the pulmonary capillaries into the pulmonary interstitium and then floods the alveolar spaces. The end-inspiratory crackles are produced by the abrupt opening of small airways and alveoli that have been collapsed and sealed shut by the surrounding edematous fluid. The elevated JVP reflects the high right atrial pressure that results from the expanded intravascular volume and, in many cases, from coexisting pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular strain.</p><h3 id=\"cutaneous-signs\">Cutaneous Signs</h3><ul><li><strong>Petechiae and Purpura:</strong> Tiny, non-blanching, red or purple macules (petechiae, &lt;3mm) or larger patches (purpura) caused by extravasation of red blood cells from capillaries into the skin. In renal patients, the primary mechanism is <strong>uremic platelet dysfunction</strong>. Uremic toxins reversibly impair platelet adhesion and aggregation, by inhibiting the binding of von Willebrand factor (vWF) to the platelet glycoprotein GPIb receptor, and by impairing the release of thromboxane A2 and ADP. This qualitative platelet defect, combined with a normal platelet count, creates a bleeding diathesis.</li><li><strong>Livedo Reticularis:</strong> A net-like, violaceous, or reddish-blue mottling of the skin, forming a complete, closed-circulation pattern. It is a sign of cholesterol crystal embolization (atheroembolic renal disease), where atherosclerotic plaques in the aorta shed cholesterol crystals that lodge in small dermal arteries, causing microvascular obstruction and ischemia.</li><li><strong>Calciphylaxis (Calcific Uremic Arteriolopathy):</strong> An exquisitely painful, devastating condition characterized by retiform purpura and painful, indurated, livedoid plaques that rapidly progress to non-healing, necrotic, black eschar ulcers, typically on the thighs, abdomen, and buttocks. The pathogenesis is the medial calcification and thrombotic occlusion of dermal arterioles and venules in the setting of advanced CKD, hyperparathyroidism, a high calcium-phosphate product, and often warfarin use.</li><li><strong>Nail Changes:</strong> In nephrotic syndrome, the nails may show <strong>Muehrcke&#39;s lines</strong>—paired, transverse, white bands parallel to the lunula, that do not move with nail growth, caused by severe, chronic hypoalbuminemia. <strong>Lindsay&#39;s nails (half-and-half nails)</strong> are seen in chronic uremia: the proximal portion of the nail is white, and the distal 20-60% is a reddish-brown band.</li></ul><h3 id=\"arteriovenous-av-fistula-signs\">Arteriovenous (AV) Fistula Signs</h3><p>In a hemodialysis patient, the AV fistula is the lifeline. Its examination is mandatory.</p><ul><li><strong>Inspection:</strong> A well-developed fistula appears as a large, tortuous, pulsatile subcutaneous vessel on the forearm or upper arm. Look for redness, swelling, or skin breakdown over the fistula site, indicating infection. Aneurysmal dilation is common.</li><li><strong>Palpation:</strong> The normal fistula has a continuous, prominent <strong>thrill</strong> (a buzzing, vibratory sensation) palpated over the venous outflow. The thrill is most intense during systole but is continuous. A loss of the thrill, or its replacement by a mere pulse, suggests thrombosis of the fistula.</li><li><strong>Auscultation:</strong> The normal fistula produces a continuous, machinery-like <strong>bruit</strong>, a harsh, rumbling sound. The intensity of the bruit should be loudest at the anastomosis and diminish as one ascends the arm.</li><li><strong>Steal Syndrome:</strong> Paleness, coldness, and pain in the hand distal to the fistula, with diminished radial pulse, caused by the fistula diverting arterial blood away from the hand.</li></ul><h3 id=\"fundoscopic-findings\">Fundoscopic Findings</h3><p>Examination of the optic fundi is a mandatory component of a renal patient&#39;s examination. It provides a direct, non-invasive view of small arteries and arterioles.</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertensive Retinopathy:</strong> In acute malignant hypertension, the fundus shows bilateral <strong>optic disc edema</strong> (papilledema), which is the defining feature of malignant hypertension. There are scattered retinal flame-shaped hemorrhages (from ruptured capillaries in the nerve fiber layer) and hard exudates (lipid deposits). A &quot;macular star&quot; figure, formed by hard exudates radiating from the fovea, is characteristic. Chronic hypertension causes arteriovenous nipping (narrowing of venules at points where they are crossed by rigid, sclerotic arterioles), copper-wiring (increased light reflex of the thickened arteriolar wall), and silver-wiring (total sclerosis).</li><li><strong>Diabetic Retinopathy:</strong> Microaneurysms, dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots (retinal nerve fiber layer infarcts), and neovascularization.</li></ul><h3 id=\"uremic-breath-fetor-uremicus\">Uremic Breath (Fetor Uremicus)</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> A characteristic ammoniacal or fishy odor to the breath. It is a clinical sign of severe, advanced uremia.</p><p><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Urea, present in very high concentrations in the plasma of uremic patients, diffuses into the saliva. The normal oral flora, particularly gram-negative anaerobes, produce the enzyme <strong>urease</strong>, which catalyzes the hydrolysis of salivary urea into ammonia (NH₃) and carbon dioxide (CO₂). The volatile ammonia gas is released in the expired air, producing the characteristic foul odor.</p>"},{"معرّف":"third-clinical-examination-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Third: Clinical Examination of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<p>The renal physical examination is never limited to the abdomen and flanks. It is a systemic, comprehensive assessment of volume status and the cardiovascular system, which is the primary pathophysiological interface with kidney disease, followed by a focused abdominal, genitourinary, and vascular examination. The sequence is structured and intentional.</p><h3 id=\"a-general-inspection-and-volume-status-assessment-begin-at-the-end-of-the-bed\">A. General Inspection and Volume Status Assessment (Begin at the End of the Bed)</h3><ul><li><strong>General Appearance:</strong> The first moments are spent observing the patient without their knowledge. Assess the level of consciousness. Is the patient alert and oriented, or is there a clouding of consciousness, lethargy, or confusion suggesting advanced uremic encephalopathy? Observe the posture. Is the patient lying still, or are they restless, writhing, and unable to find a comfortable position (suggestive of ureteric colic)? Note the body habitus. Cachexia and loss of muscle mass suggest chronic protein-energy wasting.</li><li><strong>Apparent Distress:</strong> Look for Kussmaul breathing—deep, sighing, air-hungry respirations—which is a sign of severe metabolic acidosis (often seen in advanced uremia or diabetic ketoacidosis). Observe for the flapping tremor of asterixis when the patient extends their hands.</li><li><strong>Visible Aids:</strong> Note the presence of supplemental oxygen (nasal cannula, mask), which may indicate pulmonary edema. Note any urinary catheter bag and the color and volume of the urine within it (bloody, cloudy, concentrated). Note IV lines and any infusions running.</li><li><strong>Skin:</strong> Inspect the skin surface. Is there the fine, white powder of uremic frost on the face? Are there extensive scratch marks (excoriations) from uremic pruritus? Look for the dry, scaly skin of uremic xerosis. Are there any purpuric lesions or areas of black necrosis (calciphylaxis)? Note the presence of a dialysis catheter at the neck (internal jugular vein) or an AV fistula bandage on an arm.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b-vital-signs\">B. Vital Signs</h3><ul><li><strong>Blood Pressure:</strong> Measure blood pressure with meticulous technique. The patient should be seated calmly with their back supported and feet flat on the floor for at least 5 minutes. Use an appropriately sized cuff. Measure in both arms at the initial visit and in subsequent visits if the reading is elevated. A significant difference (&gt;15 mmHg) in systolic pressure between arms raises the suspicion of subclavian artery stenosis, which would compromise flow in an ipsilateral AV fistula. Check for <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong>: measure BP after the patient has been supine for 5 minutes, then measure again after standing for 1 and 3 minutes. A drop in systolic BP of &gt;20 mmHg or diastolic BP &gt;10 mmHg indicates hypovolemia or autonomic neuropathy (common in long-standing diabetes and advanced uremia).</li><li><strong>Heart Rate:</strong> Tachycardia may be a compensatory response to hypovolemia or anemia. Bradycardia can be a sign of severe, life-threatening hyperkalemia.</li><li><strong>Respiratory Rate:</strong> Count the respiratory rate over a full minute. Tachypnea is seen in pulmonary edema, acidosis, and sepsis. Kussmaul respirations (deep, sighing breaths at a rate of &gt;20/min) indicate severe metabolic acidosis.</li><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Fever is critical. It suggests an infected renal cyst, acute pyelonephritis, dialysis catheter-associated bloodstream infection, or a complication of immunosuppressive therapy.</li></ul><h3 id=\"c-face-and-eyes\">C. Face and Eyes</h3><ul><li><strong>Periorbital Edema:</strong> Inspect the loose, distensible tissues around the eyes. Periorbital edema is most prominent upon waking and may resolve over the day. It is a classic early sign of nephrotic syndrome, due to the low-tissue-pressure in the eyelids accommodating fluid accumulation during recumbency.</li><li><strong>Conjunctival Pallor:</strong> Gently pull down the lower eyelid and inspect the mucous membrane. The color should be a healthy, moist pink. A pale, washed-out appearance indicates anemia. This is an essential part of the examination.</li><li><strong>Malar Rash:</strong> Look for the characteristic butterfly-shaped erythematous rash across the cheeks and bridge of the nose, sparing the nasolabial folds, which is a hallmark of systemic lupus erythematosus.</li><li><strong>Oral Cavity:</strong> Inspect the mucous membranes for the moisture level (dryness indicates hypovolemia) and for the presence of aphthous ulcers (seen in lupus and Behçet&#39;s disease). The breath may have the ammoniacal odor of uremia.</li></ul><h3 id=\"d-cardiovascular-examination\">D. Cardiovascular Examination</h3><ul><li><strong>Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP):</strong> This is the single most important bedside test of volume status. Position the patient so the trunk is elevated to a 45-degree angle, with the head supported on a pillow and turned slightly to the left. The internal jugular vein is identified by its characteristic biphasic, non-palpable pulsation running between the two heads of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. The vertical height of the top of the pulsation column above the sternal angle of Louis is measured using a ruler. A JVP &gt;3 cm vertically above the sternal angle indicates elevated central venous pressure and volume overload. In extreme volume overload, the JVP may only be visible when the patient is sitting upright. A flat JVP (not visible with the patient supine) indicates hypovolemia. The <strong>abdominojugular reflux test</strong> (hepatojugular reflux) is performed by applying firm, sustained pressure over the right upper quadrant for 10-15 seconds and observing the JVP. A sustained rise of &gt;3 cm during compression that falls promptly on release is a highly specific sign of right ventricular failure and volume overload.</li></ul><h3 id=\"cardiac-auscultation\">Cardiac Auscultation:</h3><ul><li><em>S3 Gallop:</em> A low-pitched, early diastolic filling sound heard best at the apex with the bell of the stethoscope. In a patient with renal disease, a ventricular S3 gallop is a sign of volume overload and left ventricular failure. It is produced by the rapid deceleration of a large volume of blood entering a non-compliant, volume-overloaded left ventricle.</li><li><em>Pericardial Friction Rub:</em> A grating, to-and-fro, leathery sound heard best at the left lower sternal border with the diaphragm, with the patient leaning forward and breath held in expiration. This is the hallmark of uremic pericarditis, a life-threatening condition requiring urgent dialysis.</li></ul><h3 id=\"e-pulmonary-examination\">E. Pulmonary Examination</h3><ul><li><strong>Inspection:</strong> Observe the respiratory pattern. Kussmaul breathing (deep, sighing, regular respirations) is a classic sign of metabolic acidosis from uremia. Cheyne-Stokes respiration (cycles of crescendo-decrescendo breathing followed by apnea) can be seen in advanced heart failure and uremia.</li><li><strong>Percussion:</strong> Percuss the posterior chest. Dullness to percussion over the lung bases, especially if it changes with position, indicates a pleural effusion, a common finding in volume overload and nephrotic syndrome.</li><li><strong>Auscultation:</strong> Listen at the posterior lung bases and mid-zones. Fine, end-inspiratory crackles (crepitations) that do not clear with a cough indicate pulmonary edema. In nephrotic syndrome, pulmonary edema can occur at relatively lower filling pressures due to the reduced plasma oncotic pressure, making the lungs more vulnerable to fluid transudation.</li></ul><h3 id=\"f-abdominal-examination\">F. Abdominal Examination</h3><p>The patient must be lying supine, with arms resting at the sides, with adequate exposure from the xiphisternum to the pubic symphysis. The bladder should ideally be empty.</p><ul><li><strong>Inspection:</strong> Look for visible flank masses (polycystic kidneys, large renal tumor). Observe for abdominal distension (ascites). Note any surgical scars (previous nephrectomy, renal transplant scar in the iliac fossa). Look for distended superficial abdominal veins (&quot;caput medusae&quot;), which can be seen in inferior vena cava obstruction.</li><li><strong>Auscultation:</strong> Before any palpation or percussion, auscultate the abdomen for <strong>renal artery bruits</strong>. Place the bell of the stethoscope 2-3 cm superior and lateral to the umbilicus, on each side. A continuous, high-pitched, systolic-diastolic bruit suggests turbulent blood flow through a hemodynamically significant renal artery stenosis. A systolic bruit alone is less specific. The absence of a bruit does not rule out renal artery stenosis.</li></ul><h3 id=\"palpation\">Palpation:</h3><ul><li><em>Technique of Kidney Palpation (Bimanual Deep Palpation/Ballottement):</em> The examiner stands on the patient&#39;s right side. For the <strong>left kidney</strong>, the examiner&#39;s left hand reaches across and under the patient&#39;s back, placing the fingers in the left flank (costophrenic angle) and lifting upward. The right hand is placed flat on the anterior abdominal wall in the left upper quadrant, lateral to the rectus muscle. The patient is asked to take a deep breath in. During inspiration, the diaphragm descends, pushing the kidney downward. As the patient exhales, the examiner attempts to capture the kidney between the two hands. A normal kidney is rarely palpable. A palpable kidney is felt as a smooth, firm organ that descends with inspiration. If a mass is palpable, determine its size, contour (smooth vs. bosselated/nodular), consistency (firm, hard, cystic, fluctuant), and tenderness. A ballotable kidney is one felt on deep inspiration and then gently bounced back between the hands. For the <strong>right kidney</strong>, the examiner&#39;s left hand remains on the patient&#39;s back, and the right hand on the anterior abdominal wall. The right kidney is lower than the left and its lower pole may be palpable in a thin, healthy individual.</li><li><em>Palpation of the Urinary Bladder:</em> The bladder is not normally palpable unless it is distended with &gt;150-200 mL of urine. A distended bladder is felt as a smooth, firm, rounded, and dull suprapubic mass that is tender in acute retention.</li></ul><h3 id=\"percussion\">Percussion:</h3><ul><li><em>Bladder Percussion:</em> Percuss the lower abdomen, starting from the midline above the pubic symphysis and moving upward. A urine-filled bladder gives a dull note, whereas the air-filled bowel above gives a tympanitic note. The change from dull to tympanitic marks the bladder dome. This is a more reliable method than palpation for detecting bladder distension.</li><li><em>Costovertebral Angle (CVA) Tenderness:</em> This is not classical percussion, but a diagnostic test. The examiner places the palm of one hand flat over the patient&#39;s costovertebral angle. The examiner makes a fist with the other hand and delivers a firm, controlled thump to the dorsum of the hand resting on the patient&#39;s back. Do not tap the patient&#39;s bare back directly. A positive test is when the patient reports sharp pain or winces. Bilateral CVA tenderness is strongly suggestive of acute pyelonephritis or acute glomerulonephritis.</li><li><em>Shifting Dullness and Fluid Thrill:</em> These are tests for ascites. With the patient supine, percuss the abdomen from the midline (tympanitic due to floating bowel) to the flank (dull due to dependent ascitic fluid). Mark the transition point. Ask the patient to roll onto the opposite side and wait for 30 seconds. Re-percuss the now-uppermost flank. If the area that was previously dull is now tympanitic, this is shifting dullness, and it is a highly specific sign for ascites.</li></ul><h3 id=\"g-genital-examination\">G. Genital Examination</h3><p>This is a mandatory part of the complete renal examination, particularly in the evaluation of hematuria, proteinuria, or obstructive symptoms, yet it is frequently omitted due to embarrassment or time constraints. It must be performed with a chaperone present.</p><ul><li><strong>Inspection of the External Meatus:</strong> Examine the urethral meatus for its position (hypospadias, epispadias), stenosis, redness, discharge (purulent, clear, blood-stained), or blood.</li><li><strong>Scrotum:</strong> Inspect and palpate for scrotal edema, which can be a striking feature of nephrotic syndrome. Testicular atrophy can be a side effect of cyclophosphamide therapy.</li><li><strong>Pelvic Examination (Women):</strong> May be necessary to evaluate for pelvic masses causing ureteral obstruction, to assess for pelvic organ prolapse contributing to bladder outlet obstruction, or to examine for a vesicovaginal fistula causing incontinence.</li></ul><h3 id=\"h-rectal-examination-digital-rectal-examination-dre\">H. Rectal Examination (Digital Rectal Examination, DRE)</h3><p>This is an essential component of the evaluation of men with lower urinary tract symptoms, hematuria, or suspected bladder outlet obstruction. The patient is placed in the left lateral decubitus position, with the hips and knees flexed.</p><ul><li><strong>Prostate Assessment:</strong> The gloved, lubricated index finger is inserted into the anus. The prostate is palpated anteriorly through the anterior rectal wall. The normal prostate is approximately the size of a walnut (3-4 cm in diameter), smooth, firm, rubbery, non-tender, with a palpable median sulcus separating the two lateral lobes. In <strong>benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)</strong> , the prostate is symmetrically enlarged, smooth, firm, and rubbery; the median sulcus may be obliterated. The rectal mucosa moves freely over it. In <strong>prostatic carcinoma</strong>, the gland is often asymmetrically enlarged, with a hard, nodular, irregular, stone-like consistency; the median sulcus may be distorted. The rectal mucosa may feel fixed to the gland in locally advanced disease. A tender, fluctuant prostate suggests acute prostatitis, a condition that requires extremely gentle examination to avoid inducing bacteremia.</li><li><strong>Anal Sphincter Tone:</strong> Assess the resting tone of the external anal sphincter and ask the patient to squeeze. A patulous anus or weak voluntary squeeze suggests a sacral nerve (S2-S4) lesion, which may be the cause of a neurogenic bladder.</li></ul><h3 id=\"i-extremity-examination\">I. Extremity Examination</h3><ul><li><strong>Pitting Edema:</strong> Press firmly with the thumb or index finger over the distal tibia for at least 5 seconds. If pitting is present, a persistent indentation will remain. Grade the severity from 1+ to 4+. In ambulatory patients, it is present in the lower extremities. In bed-bound patients, it shifts to the sacrum.</li><li><strong>AV Fistula or Graft Assessment (The Lifeline of a Dialysis Patient):</strong> This is a highly specialized and critical skill. Systematically apply: <strong>Look, Listen, Feel.</strong><ul><li><em>Inspection:</em> Look for a well-matured, visibly dilated vein. Inspect the overlying skin for erythema, induration, or pus (infection). Look for aneurysmal dilation or pseudoaneurysm. Look for the needle puncture sites.</li><li><em>Palpation:</em> First, palpate the fistula for warmth. Then, palpate the pulse. A normal fistula has a soft, easily compressible, bounding pulse. A &quot;water-hammer&quot; pulse suggests downstream stenosis. Next, palpate for the <strong>thrill</strong>. A normal thrill is a continuous, buzzing vibration. A strong, localized thrill is normal, but a new, high-frequency thrill is turbulent and may indicate stenosis. The absence of a thrill is an emergency, suggesting acute thrombosis.</li><li><em>Auscultation:</em> Listen with the bell of the stethoscope over the entire fistula. The normal <strong>bruit</strong> is continuous, with a louder systolic component. A localized, high-pitched, discontinuous bruit indicates stenosis. A loss of bruit indicates thrombosis.</li><li><em>Steal Syndrome:</em> Ask the patient to elevate the arm. If the hand becomes pale and painful (ischemic steal), this indicates that the fistula is diverting arterial blood away from the hand. Check capillary refill in the fingers.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Gouty Tophi:</strong> Inspect the joints, especially the first metatarsophalangeal joint, the ankles, the olecranon bursa, and the helix of the ear for tophi—firm, nodular deposits of monosodium urate crystals. They are a sign of chronic, poorly controlled gout, which is common in renal patients due to hyperuricemia.</li></ul><h3 id=\"j-fundoscopy\">J. Fundoscopy</h3><p>This is a non-negotiable part of the evaluation of a hypertensive or diabetic renal patient. Dilate the pupils if necessary and safe. Systematically examine the optic disc for papilledema, the arteries and veins for arteriovenous nipping and caliber changes, and the retinal background for hemorrhages, exudates, and microaneurysms. The presence of diabetic retinopathy in a proteinuric patient with diabetes strongly suggests that diabetic nephropathy is the cause of the CKD.</p><h3 id=\"k-lymph-node-examination\">K. Lymph Node Examination</h3><p>Palpate the cervical, supraclavicular (especially left, Virchow&#39;s node), axillary, and inguinal lymph nodes. Lymphadenopathy may be a clue to an underlying systemic infection, vasculitis, or malignancy (e.g., lymphoma-associated membranous nephropathy, or metastatic cancer causing obstructive uropathy).</p>"},{"معرّف":"fourth-investigations-for-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Fourth: Investigations for the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<h3 id=\"a-blood-tests\">A. Blood Tests</h3><ul><li><strong>Serum Creatinine (Cr):</strong> The most commonly used endogenous biomarker for kidney function. Creatinine is a spontaneous, non-enzymatic breakdown product of creatine and creatine phosphate in skeletal muscle. It is produced at a relatively constant daily rate, proportional to total muscle mass. It is freely filtered at the glomerulus and is not reabsorbed. However, it is <strong>actively secreted</strong> by the proximal tubular cells via the organic cation transporter (OCT2). This tubular secretion means that creatinine clearance consistently overestimates true GFR. In advanced CKD, the fractional tubular secretion increases, so serum creatinine is an increasingly inaccurate marker. Drugs such as cimetidine, trimethoprim, and the novel HIV integrase inhibitor dolutegravir competitively block the tubular secretion of creatinine, causing a mild, reversible, and non-pathological rise in serum creatinine without any change in the true GFR. This must be recognized to avoid misdiagnosis of AKI. Normal range: 0.6-1.2 mg/dL in men, 0.5-1.1 mg/dL in women.⁷</li><li><strong>Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR):</strong> GFR is not measured directly in clinical practice but is estimated using equations based on serum creatinine. The <strong>CKD-EPI 2021 creatinine equation</strong> is the current gold standard recommended by KDIGO. It uses age, sex, and serum creatinine, and the updated version has removed the race coefficient. A more accurate estimation is obtained using both serum creatinine and serum cystatin C (CKD-EPI 2021 creatinine-cystatin C equation). The eGFR is reported in mL/min/1.73 m² of body surface area. For patients of African ancestry, no coefficient is applied to the 2021 equation. The eGFR is used to stage CKD: G1 (≥90, with other markers of kidney damage), G2 (60-89), G3a (45-59), G3b (30-44), G4 (15-29), and G5 (&lt;15, kidney failure).⁷</li><li><strong>Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN):</strong> Urea is the primary nitrogenous end-product of protein and amino acid metabolism, produced in the liver via the urea cycle. It is freely filtered and variably reabsorbed in the collecting duct (enhanced in states of low tubular flow and ADH). The BUN:Creatinine ratio (normally 10-15:1) is a classic diagnostic tool. A ratio <strong>&gt;20:1</strong> suggests pre-renal azotemia. In hypoperfusion, the reduced tubular flow rate allows prolonged contact time, and ADH promotes passive urea reabsorption in the collecting duct. Elevated protein catabolism (gastrointestinal bleeding, high-dose steroids, high-protein nutrition) also raises BUN. A ratio <strong>&lt;10:1</strong> suggests intrinsic renal disease (where tubular reabsorption is impaired), malnutrition, or severe liver disease (impaired urea synthesis). Normal BUN is 7-20 mg/dL.</li><li><strong>Serum Cystatin C:</strong> A 13-kDa cysteine protease inhibitor produced at a constant rate by all nucleated cells. It is freely filtered at the glomerulus. It is then completely reabsorbed and catabolized by proximal tubular cells. It is not secreted. Its serum level is a more reliable reflection of GFR than creatinine, as it is independent of muscle mass, age, sex, and race. It is particularly useful in patients with abnormal muscle mass (elderly, cachectic, amputees, bodybuilders) or when a more accurate GFR is needed.</li></ul><h3 id=\"serum-electrolytes\">Serum Electrolytes:</h3><ul><li><em>Sodium (Na⁺):</em> 135-145 mEq/L. The master indicator of water balance. Hypernatremia (water loss) or hyponatremia (water excess or sodium loss) must be urgently assessed.</li><li><em>Potassium (K⁺):</em> 3.5-5.0 mEq/L. The most immediately life-threatening value. The renal patient&#39;s ability to excrete a potassium load is impaired. Hyperkalemia can be rapidly fatal.</li><li><em>Chloride (Cl⁻):</em> 98-106 mEq/L. Calculated in the anion gap.</li><li><em>Bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻):</em> 22-28 mEq/L. A low HCO₃⁻ indicates metabolic acidosis, a hallmark of advanced CKD, contributing to muscle wasting and bone disease.</li></ul><h3 id=\"calcium-and-phosphate\">Calcium and Phosphate:</h3><ul><li><em>Total serum calcium:</em> 8.5-10.5 mg/dL. Must be corrected for hypoalbuminemia: Corrected Ca²⁺ = Measured Ca²⁺ + 0.8 × (4.0 - patient&#39;s albumin).</li><li><em>Phosphate:</em> 2.5-4.5 mg/dL. In CKD, phosphate retention is a primary driver of secondary hyperparathyroidism, FGF23 elevation, vascular calcification, and mortality. Hyperphosphatemia is a major therapeutic target.</li><li><em>Calcium-Phosphate Product (Ca × P):</em> If &gt;55 mg²/dL², the risk of metastatic (vascular and soft tissue) calcification increases dramatically. This is the basis for calciphylaxis.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Serum Albumin:</strong> A key marker in nephrotic syndrome. A serum albumin &lt;3.0 g/dL, particularly &lt;2.0 g/dL, confirms severe hypoalbuminemia and a hypercoagulable state.</li><li><strong>Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP), Urine Protein Electrophoresis (UPEP), and Immunofixation:</strong> These are sent to detect a monoclonal (M) spike, which is diagnostic of multiple myeloma or AL amyloidosis, both of which cause renal disease. A monoclonal light chain in the urine is a Bence-Jones protein.</li></ul><h3 id=\"serology\">Serology:</h3><ul><li><em>Antinuclear Antibody (ANA) and Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA):</em> High-titer positive for lupus nephritis.</li><li><em>Complement C3 and C4 levels:</em> Low C3, normal C4: Post-streptococcal GN, C3 glomerulopathy. Low C3 and C4: Lupus nephritis, cryoglobulinemia, endocarditis. Normal C3, low C4: Cryoglobulinemia.</li><li><em>ANCA (Anti-Neutrophil Cytoplasmic Antibodies):</em> MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) or PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) for ANCA-associated vasculitis.</li><li><em>Anti-GBM (Anti-Glomerular Basement Membrane) antibodies:</em> Pathognomonic for Goodpasture&#39;s disease.</li><li><em>Anti-Phospholipase A2 Receptor (Anti-PLA2R) antibodies:</em> High-titer positive in 70-80% of cases of primary membranous nephropathy. Its titer correlates with disease activity and response to therapy.</li><li><em>Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), Hepatitis C antibody (anti-HCV), HIV serology:</em> All can cause glomerulonephritis (membranous nephropathy with HBV, membranoproliferative GN with HCV, HIVAN with HIV).</li></ul><h3 id=\"b-urine-studies\">B. Urine Studies</h3><p>The urinalysis is the single most informative, non-invasive, and underutilized test in nephrology. It must be performed on a freshly voided, clean-catch, midstream specimen and analyzed within 1-2 hours.</p><h3 id=\"dipstick-urinalysis\">Dipstick Urinalysis:</h3><ul><li><em>Specific Gravity:</em> Normal 1.003-1.030. In advanced CKD, the kidney loses its concentrating and diluting ability, and the specific gravity becomes &quot;fixed&quot; at 1.010 (isosthenuria), the same as plasma.</li><li><em>pH:</em> Normal 4.5-8.0. A consistently alkaline urine (pH &gt;7) suggests a renal tubular acidosis (distal RTA) or an infection with a urease-producing organism (<em>Proteus</em>, <em>Klebsiella</em>).</li><li><em>Blood (Heme):</em> The dipstick is highly sensitive for the heme moiety, detecting free hemoglobin (from intravascular hemolysis) and myoglobin (from rhabdomyolysis), as well as intact red blood cells. A positive dipstick for blood that is negative for RBCs on microscopy raises the diagnosis of rhabdomyolysis or hemolysis.</li><li><em>Protein:</em> The dipstick is semi-quantitative (trace, 1+, 2+, 3+, 4+). It is specific for <strong>albumin</strong>. It is insensitive to non-albumin proteins (immunoglobulin light chains in myeloma).</li><li><em>Glucose:</em> Glucosuria on dipstick with a normal blood glucose suggests proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome). In SGLT2 inhibitor therapy, glucosuria is an expected pharmacological effect.</li><li><em>Ketones:</em> Positive in diabetic ketoacidosis, starvation, and the euglycemic DKA of SGLT2 inhibitors.</li><li><em>Leukocyte Esterase:</em> An enzyme present in neutrophils. A positive result indicates pyuria (WBCs in the urine).</li><li><em>Nitrite:</em> Produced by the reduction of dietary nitrates to nitrites by gram-negative bacteria (especially <em>E. coli</em>). A positive nitrite test is highly specific for bacteriuria.¹</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Urine Microscopy (Examination of the Spun Sediment):</strong> This is the &quot;liquid biopsy&quot; of the kidney. A fresh, 10-15 mL sample is centrifuged, the supernatant poured off, and the pellet re-suspended and examined under a coverslip with light microscopy and a polarizer.<ul><li><em>Red Blood Cells (RBCs):</em> Normal: 0-2 RBCs per high-power field (HPF). <strong>Dysmorphic RBCs</strong> (fragmented, irregular, bleb-like), especially <strong>acanthocytes</strong> (ring-shaped RBCs with blebs), are highly specific for glomerular bleeding. <strong>Isomorphic RBCs</strong> suggest a non-glomerular source.²</li><li><em>Red Blood Cell Casts:</em> These are cylindrical bodies formed by Tamm-Horsfall protein in the distal tubule, which traps RBCs. They are virtually <strong>pathognomonic for glomerulonephritis</strong>. Their presence is a renal emergency.</li><li><em>White Blood Cells (WBCs):</em> Normal: 0-4 WBCs/HPF. &gt;5 WBCs/HPF indicates pyuria, which can be due to infection or acute interstitial nephritis (AIN).</li><li><em>WBC Casts:</em> Pathognomonic for acute interstitial nephritis or acute pyelonephritis, indicating inflammation originating in the renal parenchyma, not the lower tract.</li><li><em>Muddy Brown Granular Casts:</em> These are coarse, granular casts composed of degenerated renal tubular epithelial cells and cellular debris. They are the pathognomonic finding in <strong>acute tubular necrosis (ATN)</strong> .</li><li><em>Hyaline Casts:</em> Composed of Tamm-Horsfall protein. They are non-specific and can be seen in normal concentrated urine, after exercise, or during diuretic therapy.</li><li><em>Fatty Casts and Oval Fat Bodies:</em> Degenerating tubular cells that have absorbed lipids and cholesterol esters. They appear as &quot;Maltese crosses&quot; under polarized light. They are characteristic of heavy proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome.</li><li><em>Crystals:</em> Calcium oxalate (envelope-shaped), uric acid (pleomorphic rhomboids), struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate, &quot;coffin-lid&quot; shaped, associated with infection), cystine (hexagonal plates, pathognomonic for cystinuria).</li></ul></li></ul><h3 id=\"quantitative-proteinuria\">Quantitative Proteinuria:</h3><ul><li><em>Spot Urine Protein-to-Creatinine Ratio (UPCR) or Albumin-to-Creatinine Ratio (UACR):</em> A random spot urine sample is currently the preferred method for quantifying proteinuria. It correlates well with the 24-hour collection. Normal UACR is &lt;30 mg/g. Microalbuminuria (moderately increased albuminuria) is 30-300 mg/g. Macroalbuminuria (severely increased albuminuria) is &gt;300 mg/g. Nephrotic-range proteinuria is &gt;3.5 g/g (UPCR &gt;3500 mg/g).</li></ul><h3 id=\"urine-osmolality-and-electrolytes\">Urine Osmolality and Electrolytes:</h3><ul><li><em>Urine Sodium (U_Na) and Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa):</em> Used in the differential diagnosis of oliguric AKI. In a pre-renal state, the kidney avidly retains sodium, and U_Na is &lt;20 mEq/L, and FENa is &lt;1%. In ATN, the damaged tubules cannot reabsorb sodium, so U_Na is &gt;40 mEq/L and FENa &gt;2%. FENa must be interpreted with caution in patients taking diuretics, which elevate urine sodium.</li><li><em>Fractional Excretion of Urea (FEUrea):</em> Used in patients on diuretics. FEUrea &lt;35% suggests a pre-renal cause.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Urine Culture:</strong> The gold standard for diagnosing a urinary tract infection. A count of ≥10⁵ colony-forming units (CFU)/mL of a single organism is the classic definition of a positive culture for a midstream urine specimen.</li></ul><h3 id=\"c-imaging\">C. Imaging</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal and Bladder Ultrasonography (Ultrasound):</strong> This is the first-line, non-invasive imaging modality in nephrology. It provides essential anatomical information without radiation or iodinated contrast. It assesses kidney size (normal adult size 9-13 cm in length), cortical thickness and echogenicity (increased echogenicity indicates fibrosis and chronic damage), and the presence or absence of hydronephrosis (dilated, fluid-filled collecting system, indicating obstruction). A normal renal ultrasound effectively rules out significant chronic disease and is the best test to rule out post-renal obstruction. It can detect simple and complex cysts, solid tumors, and nephrolithiasis. A bladder ultrasound measures post-void residual urine volume.</li><li><strong>Doppler Ultrasound of the Renal Arteries:</strong> Used to screen for renal artery stenosis. It measures the peak systolic velocity in the renal artery. A velocity &gt;180-200 cm/s and a renal-to-aortic ratio &gt;3.5 suggest significant stenosis.</li><li><strong>Non-Contrast CT Scan (NCCT):</strong> The gold standard investigation for the detection of renal and ureteric calculi. It has a sensitivity and specificity of &gt;95%. It can detect virtually all stone types (including radiolucent uric acid stones) and shows signs of obstruction (hydronephrosis, perinephric stranding).</li><li><strong>CT Urography (CT-IVP):</strong> A contrast-enhanced CT with delayed images to opacify the collecting system. It is the gold standard for evaluating the urothelium in the workup of hematuria, looking for transitional cell carcinoma. It requires iodine-based IV contrast, which is nephrotoxic.</li><li><strong>Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA):</strong> The non-invasive gold standard for diagnosing renal artery stenosis. Gadolinium-based contrast agents are absolutely contraindicated in patients with an eGFR &lt;30 mL/min/1.73 m² due to the risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis (NSF), a devastating, irreversible, and often fatal fibrosing disease of the skin and internal organs.</li></ul><h3 id=\"d-percutaneous-renal-biopsy\">D. Percutaneous Renal Biopsy</h3><p>This is the definitive, gold standard investigation for the diagnosis of parenchymal renal disease (glomerular disease, unexplained AKI). It is performed under real-time ultrasound guidance using a spring-loaded biopsy needle. The tissue core is examined in three ways: light microscopy (to assess glomerular, tubular, interstitial, and vascular morphology), immunofluorescence (to detect the deposition of immunoglobulins—IgG, IgA, IgM—and complement components—C3, C1q—using fluorescently-labeled antibodies), and electron microscopy (for the ultrastructural localization of immune deposits, assessment of the GBM thickness, and visualization of podocyte foot process effacement).¹⁰</p>"},{"معرّف":"fifth-how-to-assess-the-system-clinically-and-ensure-it-is-healthy","عنوان":"Fifth: How to Assess the System Clinically and Ensure It Is Healthy","html":"<p>A proactive, systematic approach using history, physical examination, and screening investigations can identify kidney disease in its early, asymptomatic stages, when intervention is most effective.</p><h3 id=\"a-history\">A. History</h3><p>Actively screen for the major, modifiable risk factors for CKD.</p><ul><li><strong>Diabetes Mellitus:</strong> The single leading cause of ESRD worldwide. Ask about the duration, control (HbA1c), and presence of other microvascular complications (retinopathy, neuropathy).</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> The second most common cause. Ask about the duration, number of medications, and control.</li><li><strong>Family History:</strong> A detailed family history is critical. Ask specifically about &quot;kidney disease requiring dialysis or transplantation&quot; in first-degree relatives. ADPKD is autosomal dominant; Alport syndrome is X-linked. A positive family history is a strong risk factor.</li><li><strong>Medication History (Nephrotoxins):</strong> A detailed, lifetime review of all medications, including over-the-counter and traditional remedies. Specifically ask about chronic NSAID use, which causes analgesic nephropathy. Review exposure to antibiotics (aminoglycosides, vancomycin), chemotherapy (cisplatin), and proton pump inhibitors (associated with acute interstitial nephritis).</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Disease:</strong> The risk factors for CKD and CVD are shared. The presence of coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease, or cerebrovascular disease increases the pretest probability of CKD.</li><li><strong>Autoimmune Disease History:</strong> Ask about symptoms of systemic lupus erythematosus (joint pain, skin rashes, pleurisy) or systemic vasculitis (sinusitis, hemoptysis, skin purpura).</li><li><strong>History of Infections:</strong> Previous episodes of pyelonephritis, recurrent cystitis, or a history of hepatitis B, hepatitis C, or HIV infection.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b-physical-examination\">B. Physical Examination</h3><p>To ensure the renal system is healthy, one must demonstrate the absence of the signs detailed above. The normal examination should document: normotensive blood pressure; no periorbital or peripheral edema; no pulmonary crackles; a normal JVP; no audible renal artery bruits; non-palpable, non-tender kidneys; no costovertebral angle tenderness; and a normally functioning AV fistula (if present) with a continuous thrill and bruit.</p><h3 id=\"c-screening-investigations\">C. Screening Investigations</h3><ul><li><strong>Blood Pressure:</strong> Accurate measurement at every clinical encounter.</li><li><strong>Serum Creatinine and eGFR:</strong> According to the KDIGO 2024 guideline, annual screening for CKD with serum creatinine and eGFR is recommended for all individuals at high risk: those with diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, a family history of kidney failure, or age &gt;60 years.⁷</li><li><strong>Urinalysis and Urine Albumin-to-Creatinine Ratio (UACR):</strong> All patients with diabetes must undergo annual screening for albuminuria. In type 1 diabetes, screening begins 5 years after diagnosis. In type 2 diabetes, screening begins at the time of diagnosis. Annual UACR measurement is also recommended in all patients with hypertension. A UACR &gt;30 mg/g is abnormal and requires confirmation on two of three samples over 3-6 months.</li><li><strong>Diagnosis of CKD:</strong> According to KDIGO 2024, CKD is defined as the presence of markers of kidney damage (any of: albuminuria &gt;30 mg/g, abnormal urinary sediment, histological or imaging abnormality, or a history of kidney transplantation) <strong>OR</strong> an eGFR &lt;60 mL/min/1.73 m², present for at least <strong>3 months</strong>.¹¹ This requires a repeat eGFR and UACR after 3 months.</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"sixth-diseases-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Sixth: Diseases of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<p>A comprehensive, detailed list of the most important and most common diseases affecting the renal system, with their pathogenesis, typical clinical presentation, and the key distinguishing feature for pattern recognition.</p><h3 id=\"1-diabetic-nephropathy\">1. Diabetic Nephropathy</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Chronic, sustained hyperglycemia is the initiating injury. Glucose forms irreversible advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) with proteins in the glomerular basement membrane and mesangium. AGEs activate protein kinase C, the polyol pathway, and the transcription factor NF-κB, leading to increased production of transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β), connective tissue growth factor, and reactive oxygen species. This results in the hallmark pathological changes: <strong>mesangial expansion</strong> (increased mesangial matrix), <strong>glomerular basement membrane thickening</strong>, and <strong>podocyte injury and detachment</strong>. Hemodynamically, hyperglycemia impairs the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism, causing inappropriate afferent arteriolar vasodilation. This leads to a state of <strong>glomerular hyperfiltration</strong>—an elevated GFR and intraglomerular pressure in the early stages—which is mechanically damaging to the capillary wall and contributes to progressive proteinuria and glomerulosclerosis. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is inappropriately activated.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A patient with a 10-15 year history of type 1 or type 2 diabetes, who is found to have progressively increasing amounts of albumin in the urine. Initially, it manifests as microalbuminuria (UACR 30-300 mg/g). If untreated, this progresses over years to overt, macroalbuminuric proteinuria (&gt;300 mg/g). Serum creatinine begins a slow, relentless, linear rise. The patient invariably has concomitant <strong>diabetic retinopathy</strong> on fundoscopic examination; the absence of retinopathy in a diabetic patient with heavy proteinuria and CKD should prompt a search for an alternative, non-diabetic glomerular disease. Hypertension develops and worsens in parallel with the CKD.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The presence of <strong>diabetic retinopathy</strong> in a patient with long-standing diabetes, proteinuria, and slowly progressive CKD. It is the single most common cause of end-stage renal disease worldwide.</li></ul><h3 id=\"2-hypertensive-nephrosclerosis\">2. Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Chronic, poorly controlled essential hypertension causes structural damage to the renal microvasculature. The sustained high pressure causes a hyaline thickening of the walls of the afferent arterioles (hyaline arteriolosclerosis), due to the insudation and deposition of plasma proteins and lipids into the vessel wall. This narrows the arteriolar lumen, reducing blood flow and causing ischemic injury to the downstream glomeruli. The glomeruli undergo global sclerosis—a slow, ischemic collapse of the capillary tuft with deposition of collagen. The tubules atrophy, and the interstitium becomes fibrotic (&quot;nephrosclerosis&quot;). This is a slow, chronic process that leads to a shrunken, granular, and scarred kidney.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> An elderly patient with a 15-30 year history of primary hypertension, who has a slowly, gradually rising serum creatinine over years. Urinalysis is often bland or shows only mild albuminuria (&lt;1 g/day), but no active sediment (no RBC casts). The hypertension may be difficult to control, and this condition is significantly more common and more aggressive in individuals of African ancestry, often progressing to ESRD.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Bland urinalysis (minimal proteinuria, no hematuria, no casts) in a patient with a long history of hypertension, with slowly progressive CKD. It is a diagnosis of exclusion, but the clinical context is classic.</li></ul><h3 id=\"3-acute-tubular-necrosis-atn\">3. Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common cause of intrinsic renal AKI in hospitalized patients. It is most often caused by a combination of <strong>ischemia</strong> (prolonged prerenal state from shock, sepsis, major surgery, cardiopulmonary bypass) and <strong>nephrotoxins</strong> (aminoglycoside antibiotics, amphotericin B, cisplatin, radiocontrast agents, heme pigments from myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis). The pathogenesis is a unified process: a period of severe, sustained renal hypoperfusion and/or direct toxic insult causes ATP depletion in the tubular epithelial cells, which are highly metabolically active. This leads to loss of cell polarity, disruption of the actin cytoskeleton, and detachment of the cells from the tubular basement membrane. The detached cells and brush border fragments aggregate with Tamm-Horsfall protein, forming occlusive casts that block the tubular lumen. Concurrently, there is intense afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, reducing GFR. The tubular obstruction and back-leak of glomerular filtrate across the denuded, damaged basement membrane into the renal interstitium and circulation are the key mechanisms of the profound reduction in GFR. The tubules can regenerate from surviving cells if the basement membrane is intact.¹²</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> An acutely ill, hospitalized patient (post-operative, septic, hypotensive) who develops an abrupt rise in serum creatinine (over hours to days) and oliguria. The urine sediment is where the diagnosis is made: it reveals pathognomonic <strong>muddy brown granular casts</strong> and tubular epithelial cells. The BUN:Cr ratio is usually &lt;15:1. The FENa is &gt;2%.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The combination of a clinical setting of hypotension/sepsis/nephrotoxin exposure, oliguric AKI, and the finding of <strong>muddy brown granular casts</strong> on urine microscopy. This is the single most pathognomonic laboratory finding in all of nephrology for ATN.</li></ul><h3 id=\"4-acute-interstitial-nephritis-ain\">4. Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> An acute, cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction within the renal interstitium. The most common cause is a <strong>drug reaction</strong> (75% of cases). The prototypical drugs are beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins), proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, pantoprazole), NSAIDs, and sulfonamides. The drug or its metabolite acts as a hapten, binding to proteins in the tubular basement membrane and eliciting a delayed-type (Type IV) hypersensitivity reaction. The renal interstitium becomes edematous and heavily infiltrated with T lymphocytes, macrophages, and eosinophils. The tubules are compressed and damaged (&quot;tubulitis&quot;).</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A patient who has been taking a culprit medication for 1-3 weeks, who develops a sudden rise in serum creatinine, often with non-oliguric AKI. The classic triad is fever, maculopapular skin rash, and peripheral eosinophilia, but this triad is present in only a minority of cases (&lt;10-30%). The urine sediment shows <strong>white blood cell casts</strong> and <strong>sterile pyuria</strong> (many WBCs with a negative urine culture). Eosinophiluria, historically touted, has poor sensitivity and specificity.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The presence of <strong>WBC casts</strong> and sterile pyuria in a patient with unexplained AKI who is taking a common culprit medication. A history of PPI or antibiotic use is the most important clue.</li></ul><h3 id=\"5-post-streptococcal-glomerulonephritis-psgn\">5. Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> An immune complex-mediated disease that follows 1-4 weeks after an infection with a nephritogenic strain of group A β-hemolytic <em>Streptococcus</em> (pharyngitis or impetigo). The nephritogenic strains (e.g., M types 12, 49) produce specific antigens, such as nephritis-associated plasmin receptor (NAPlr) and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B (SpeB). The host produces antibodies against these streptococcal antigens. These antibodies form circulating immune complexes with the antigens, which then deposit in the subepithelial space of the glomerular basement membrane. These deposits activate the alternative complement pathway, leading to a consumption of C3 and the formation of characteristic large, dome-shaped subepithelial electron-dense deposits (&quot;humps&quot;) seen on electron microscopy. The complement activation attracts neutrophils and macrophages, causing a diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis.¹³</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A child of school age (peak age 5-12 years), who had a sore throat or skin infection 2-3 weeks ago, suddenly presenting with dark, tea-colored or cola-colored urine (gross hematuria), puffy periorbital edema, hypertension, and oliguria. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts. Serum complement C3 is profoundly low, while C4 is normal.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> A low serum <strong>C3</strong> complement level that returns to normal within <strong>6-8 weeks</strong>. The EM finding of <strong>subepithelial humps</strong> is pathognomonic.</li></ul><h3 id=\"6-iga-nephropathy-bergers-disease\">6. IgA Nephropathy (Berger&#39;s Disease)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common primary glomerulonephritis in the world. The fundamental defect is an abnormal O-glycosylation of the hinge region of the immunoglobulin IgA1 molecule. The hinge region contains a series of proline, serine, and threonine residues, which are normally O-glycosylated with galactose. In IgA nephropathy, there is a reduced galactosylation, leading to <strong>galactose-deficient IgA1 (Gd-IgA1)</strong> . This aberrantly glycosylated IgA1 is recognized as a foreign antigen by the host&#39;s own IgG autoantibodies (anti-glycan antibodies). The resulting Gd-IgA1-IgG immune complexes circulate, and because of their large size and affinity for mesangial matrix proteins, they lodge in the glomerular mesangium. The deposited complexes activate the mesangial cells, causing their proliferation and the overproduction of mesangial matrix and inflammatory cytokines. This leads to mesangial proliferative glomerulonephritis.¹⁴</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A young adult, most commonly a male in his 20s or 30s, presenting with recurrent episodes of macroscopic hematuria that occur simultaneously with or within 1-3 days of an upper respiratory tract or gastrointestinal mucosal infection (&quot;synpharyngitic hematuria&quot;). This is in contrast to PSGN, where hematuria occurs 1-3 weeks after the infection. Persistent microscopic hematuria and mild proteinuria may be found between episodes. Some patients present with a slowly progressive CKD and hypertension without macroscopic hematuria.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:Synpharyngitic hematuria</strong> (gross hematuria occurring during the mucosal infection). The diagnosis is confirmed by renal biopsy showing dominant or co-dominant IgA deposits in the mesangium on immunofluorescence.</li></ul><h3 id=\"7-minimal-change-disease-mcd\">7. Minimal Change Disease (MCD)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children (90% of cases in children &lt;10 years old). The glomeruli appear completely normal on light microscopy and immunofluorescence. The defining ultrastructural finding on electron microscopy is a diffuse, uniform <strong>effacement (flattening, retraction) of the podocyte foot processes</strong>. The current leading hypothesis is that it is a T-cell-mediated disorder. A circulating permeability factor, possibly hemopexin, angiopoietin-like-4, or a cytokine produced by dysfunctional T-cells, directly targets and injures the podocyte. This injury causes disorganization of the actin cytoskeleton, internalization of nephrin, and disruption of the slit diaphragm&#39;s charge and size barrier, leading to the selective, massive loss of albumin (selective proteinuria).³</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A child, typically between 2 and 8 years old, who develops the sudden, dramatic onset of severe periorbital and peripheral edema, foamy urine, and weight gain from fluid retention. Massive proteinuria (&gt;3.5 g/day, UPCR &gt;3500 mg/g) and hypoalbuminemia (serum albumin often &lt;2.0 g/dL) are present. Renal function is typically preserved, and hypertension is uncommon at presentation. The disease classically responds rapidly and dramatically to high-dose oral prednisone therapy, with complete remission of proteinuria within 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Normal light microscopy, complete foot process effacement on electron microscopy, and an exquisite, rapid response to corticosteroid therapy in a young child with pure nephrotic syndrome.</li></ul><h3 id=\"8-focal-segmental-glomerulosclerosis-fsgs\">8. Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> FSGS is not a single disease but a histological pattern of scarring that affects some but not all glomeruli (focal) and only a portion of each affected glomerulus (segmental). The primary, unifying mechanism is <strong>podocyte injury and depletion (podocytopenia)</strong> . The podocyte is a terminally differentiated cell with little capacity for regeneration. When podocytes are injured, they detach from the GBM, leaving areas of the basement membrane denuded and exposed. These bare GBM areas come into direct contact with the parietal epithelial cells of Bowman&#39;s capsule, forming an adhesion (synechia). This adhesion is the initial nidus for the entrapment of plasma proteins and the eventual deposition of fibrous, hyaline material, forming the segmental scar. The causes of podocyte injury include: a circulating permeability factor (e.g., soluble urokinase plasminogen activator receptor, suPAR) in primary FSGS; genetic mutations in podocyte proteins (nephrin, podocin, alpha-actinin-4, TRPC6) in genetic FSGS; hyperfiltration injury (secondary FSGS from obesity, reflux nephropathy, unilateral renal agenesis); and direct podocyte toxicity from viral infections (HIV, parvovirus B19) or drugs (pamidronate, anabolic steroids, heroin).¹⁵</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> In primary FSGS, it presents similarly to MCD, with an abrupt onset of nephrotic syndrome. However, it occurs more commonly in adults and older children. A key difference is that it frequently has a poorer response to steroids, progresses more aggressively to CKD, and has a high rate of recurrence in the transplanted kidney (30-50%).</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The presence of a segmental scar in some glomeruli on light microscopy, accompanied by foot process effacement on EM, in a patient with nephrotic syndrome that does not respond to steroids. Recurrence of proteinuria within hours to days after a kidney transplant is a hallmark of the primary, circulating factor form.</li></ul><h3 id=\"9-membranous-nephropathy\">9. Membranous Nephropathy</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in non-diabetic, Caucasian adults. It is an autoimmune disease where antibodies form against an antigen expressed on the podocyte cell surface. In approximately 70-80% of cases, the autoantigen is the phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R). In a smaller percentage, it is thrombospondin type-1 domain-containing 7A (THSD7A). The circulating autoantibodies (predominantly IgG4) cross the glomerular basement membrane and bind to their target antigen on the podocyte foot process. This forms subepithelial immune complexes. These deposits activate the complement cascade. The podocyte, like a gut epithelial cell, is resistant to lysis by the C5b-9 membrane attack complex (MAC). Instead, a sublytic amount of MAC inserts into the podocyte membrane and activates it, causing it to produce excessive amounts of extracellular matrix material (type IV collagen, laminin). This matrix accumulates between and around the immune deposits, creating the characteristic &quot;spikes&quot; on silver stain and progressive thickening of the GBM. The activated podocyte loses its slit diaphragm integrity, causing massive, non-selective proteinuria.¹⁶</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A middle-aged or older adult (&gt;50 years) with the insidious onset of nephrotic syndrome (severe edema, massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia). Renal function is often normal at diagnosis but declines slowly over years if untreated. A major, life-threatening complication is an acquired hypercoagulable state, leading to renal vein thrombosis, deep vein thrombosis, and pulmonary embolism, due to the urinary loss of antithrombin III. The diagnosis is confirmed by a positive serum anti-PLA2R antibody, or by renal biopsy showing the characteristic subepithelial deposits and GBM spikes.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The presence of <strong>anti-PLA2R antibodies</strong> in the serum, which are both diagnostic and prognostic. On biopsy, the hallmark is <strong>subepithelial immune deposits</strong> with GBM thickening and &quot;spike&quot; formation.</li></ul><h3 id=\"10-membranoproliferative-glomerulonephritis-mpgn-c3-glomerulopathy\">10. Membranoproliferative Glomerulonephritis (MPGN) / C3 Glomerulopathy</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> This is a pattern of glomerular injury characterized by mesangial hypercellularity and capillary wall thickening (double-contour or &quot;tram-track&quot; appearance on light microscopy). The modern classification is based on immunofluorescence, dividing it into immune-complex mediated MPGN (with immunoglobulins) and complement-mediated MPGN (C3 glomerulopathy, where C3 is dominant). C3 glomerulopathy is caused by uncontrolled, unregulated activation of the alternative pathway of the complement cascade. This can be due to a genetic mutation in a complement regulatory protein (Factor H, Factor I, MCP/CD46) or, more commonly, an acquired autoantibody that stabilizes the C3 convertase enzyme (C3 nephritic factor, C3NeF). The unopposed activation leads to the continuous cleavage of C3 and its deposition along the GBM. The classic form is Dense Deposit Disease (DDD), where electron-dense material is deposited within the lamina densa of the GBM.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A patient of any age (children, young adults, or the elderly) presenting with a mixed nephritic-nephrotic picture (proteinuria, hematuria, hypertension, and a decline in GFR). A critical laboratory feature is a <strong>persistently and profoundly low serum C3</strong> level, with a normal C4.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Persistently and severely low serum <strong>C3</strong> complement, with normal C4, and a &quot;tram-track&quot; double contour of the GBM on light microscopy.</li></ul><h3 id=\"11-lupus-nephritis\">11. Lupus Nephritis</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Renal involvement in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a classic immune complex-mediated disease. The loss of self-tolerance leads to the production of autoantibodies, particularly high-affinity anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA) antibodies. These autoantibodies can directly bind to antigens on the glomerular cells, or, more commonly, they form circulating immune complexes with their DNA and nucleosome antigens. These complexes preferentially deposit in the subendothelial (between the endothelial cell and GBM) and mesangial spaces of the glomerulus. The deposited complexes activate complement (consuming C3 and C4, causing low levels of both) and recruit inflammatory cells. The type of glomerular injury depends on the site and quantity of the deposits. The International Society of Nephrology/Renal Pathology Society (ISN/RPS) classification defines six classes, from Class I (minimal mesangial) to Class VI (advanced sclerosing). The most severe, proliferative forms (Class III: focal proliferative, and Class IV: diffuse proliferative) are characterized by heavy subendothelial deposits, endocapillary hypercellularity, crescents, and an active urine sediment.¹⁷</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A young woman (peak age 20-40 years) with known or newly diagnosed SLE (with characteristic malar rash, arthritis, serositis, photosensitivity, positive ANA, and positive high-titer anti-dsDNA antibodies), who develops proteinuria, hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, and a rise in serum creatinine. An active urinary sediment with dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts is the hallmark of active proliferative lupus nephritis.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The combination of SLE systemic symptoms, positive serology (ANA, anti-dsDNA), and <strong>low C3 AND low C4</strong> (which indicates activation of the classical pathway), and an active urine sediment with RBC casts.</li></ul><h3 id=\"12-anti-gbm-disease-goodpastures-disease\">12. Anti-GBM Disease (Goodpasture&#39;s Disease)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> This is a severe, organ-specific autoimmune disease caused by autoantibodies directed against the non-collagenous domain 1 of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen (α3(IV)NC1). This specific collagen chain is uniquely expressed in the basement membranes of the kidney glomerulus and the lung alveoli. The binding of the autoantibodies (typically IgG1 and IgG3) to the GBM causes a dramatic, linear deposition of IgG along the entire length of the GBM. This is seen as a brilliant, ribbon-like linear staining on immunofluorescence. The bound antibodies activate complement and recruit neutrophils and macrophages. This results in a devastating, necrotizing, and crescentic glomerulonephritis, where fibrin and inflammatory cells leak into Bowman&#39;s space and proliferate, forming crescents that obliterate the filtration space. This is a Rapidly Progressive Glomerulonephritis (RPGN). If the lungs are involved, the antibodies bind to the alveolar basement membrane, causing pulmonary hemorrhage.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A young adult male (classic bimodal age distribution: young men and older women) presenting with an RPGN (a rapid decline in GFR over days to weeks, with a very active urine sediment—dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts), often accompanied by hemoptysis (coughing up blood), dyspnea, and bilateral patchy alveolar infiltrates on chest X-ray (diffuse alveolar hemorrhage).</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The detection of circulating <strong>anti-GBM antibodies</strong> in the serum, and the finding of <strong>linear IgG deposition</strong> along the GBM on renal biopsy immunofluorescence. This is a medical emergency.</li></ul><h3 id=\"13-anca-associated-vasculitis-aav\">13. ANCA-Associated Vasculitis (AAV)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> A group of systemic, necrotizing, pauci-immune (minimal or no immune deposits on immunofluorescence) small-vessel vasculitides. The central pathophysiology is the presence of anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA). The two main antigens are proteinase 3 (PR3, giving a cytoplasmic ANCA pattern, c-ANCA) and myeloperoxidase (MPO, giving a perinuclear ANCA pattern, p-ANCA). The current model is that in a genetically predisposed host, an environmental trigger (infection, drug) causes the aberrant expression of MPO and PR3 on the surface of circulating neutrophils. ANCA antibodies bind to these antigens and simultaneously bind to Fc-gamma receptors on adjacent neutrophils, causing excessive, uncontrolled activation. The activated neutrophils degranulate, releasing toxic oxygen radicals, elastase, and myeloperoxidase. They also undergo a particular form of cell death called NETosis, releasing neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs), which are sticky webs of chromatin and enzymes. This causes direct, necrotizing injury to the walls of small blood vessels, including the glomerular capillaries. Since this is a direct attack on the vessel wall, there is no immune complex deposition, resulting in a &quot;pauci-immune&quot; pattern on biopsy.¹⁸</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A middle-aged or older adult with a prodrome of fever, malaise, weight loss, and arthralgias. The renal presentation is an RPGN with a very active urine sediment (dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, proteinuria). The patient may also have symptoms of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, formerly Wegener&#39;s): necrotizing sinusitis, saddle-nose deformity, chronic otitis media, cough, hemoptysis, and cavitary lung nodules. Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA) is more commonly associated with MPO-ANCA and often presents with a pulmonary-renal syndrome. Eosinophilic GPA (Churg-Strauss) presents with asthma and eosinophilia.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> A positive <strong>ANCA</strong> serology (by ELISA for PR3 or MPO) in a patient with an RPGN. The renal biopsy shows a necrotizing, crescentic GN with <strong>pauci-immune</strong> staining (no immune deposits).</li></ul><h3 id=\"14-hemolytic-uremic-syndrome-hus-and-thrombotic-thrombocytopenic-purpura-ttp\">14. Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (HUS) and Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> These are diseases of thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA), characterized by the formation of platelet-rich microthrombi in the microvasculature. In <strong>typical (Shiga toxin-producingE. coli) HUS</strong>, the Shiga toxin, released by the bacteria (<em>E. coli</em> O157:H7), binds to the globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) receptor on glomerular endothelial cells. It enters the cell and inhibits ribosomal protein synthesis, causing direct endothelial injury and death. This provokes platelet adhesion, activation, and thrombi formation. In <strong>atypical HUS (aHUS)</strong> , there is an inherited or acquired defect in the regulation of the alternative complement pathway (mutations in Factor H, Factor I, MCP/CD46, or autoantibody to Factor H), leading to uncontrolled complement activation on the endothelial surface, causing endothelial injury. In <strong>TTP</strong>, there is a congenital or acquired deficiency of the ADAMTS13 metalloprotease. ADAMTS13 is responsible for cleaving the ultra-large multimers of von Willebrand factor (vWF) that are released from endothelial cells. In its absence, these highly thrombogenic large multimers accumulate, causing spontaneous, systemic platelet aggregation and microthrombi formation.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> The classic pentad of TTP is fever, neurological symptoms (fluctuating, confusion, seizures), thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA, with schistocytes on the peripheral blood smear and elevated LDH, low haptoglobin), and AKI. In typical HUS, it follows a few days after a bout of acute, bloody diarrhea. In aHUS, it often occurs without a diarrheal prodrome.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The triad of <strong>thrombocytopenia, MAHA (schistocytes), and AKI</strong>. In TTP, the ADAMTS13 activity is &lt;10%. In HUS, it is normal. The distinction is critical because TTP is treated with plasmapheresis, while typical HUS is supportive, and aHUS is treated with complement blockade (eculizumab).</li></ul><h3 id=\"15-autosomal-dominant-polycystic-kidney-disease-adpkd\">15. Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common inherited kidney disease. Mutations in the PKD1 gene (85% of cases, on chromosome 16, encoding polycystin-1) or the PKD2 gene (15% of cases, on chromosome 4, encoding polycystin-2). These two proteins form a functional complex on the primary cilium of renal tubular epithelial cells. The primary cilium is a single, non-motile, hair-like organelle that bends with the flow of tubular fluid. The polycystin-1/2 complex acts as a mechanosensor, translating ciliary bending into a calcium influx into the cell. This intracellular calcium signal normally tonically suppresses the cAMP pathway. In ADPKD, the loss of function of this complex abolishes the calcium influx. The resulting drop in intracellular calcium leads to dysregulated, constitutive activation of adenylyl cyclase and high levels of cyclic AMP (cAMP). This abnormally high cAMP drives fluid secretion (via the CFTR chloride channel) into the cyst lumen and drives cell proliferation (via MAP kinase pathway). This leads to the progressive development and relentless enlargement of thousands of fluid-filled cysts, which eventually detach from their parent tubules and compress and destroy the surrounding normal parenchyma.¹⁹</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A young to middle-aged adult with a positive family history (parent with the disease), presenting with hypertension (the earliest and most common symptom), bilateral flank or abdominal pain from the mass effect of the enlarged kidneys, gross hematuria (from cyst rupture), and recurrent urinary tract infections. On examination, the kidneys are bilaterally and massively enlarged, palpable, and irregularly bosselated. Extra-renal manifestations include multiple hepatic cysts, intracranial berry aneurysms (risk of subarachnoid hemorrhage), and mitral valve prolapse.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The family history and the finding of <strong>bilaterally enlarged, cystic, and nodular kidneys</strong> on physical examination and renal ultrasound.</li></ul><h3 id=\"16-renal-cell-carcinoma-rcc\">16. Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The most common type (75%) is the clear cell carcinoma. It is intimately linked to a mutation in the von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 3p. The VHL protein (pVHL) is the substrate recognition component of an E3 ubiquitin ligase complex. Under normal oxygen conditions, pVHL binds to hydroxylated hypoxia-inducible factor-alpha (HIF-α) and targets it for ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. In clear cell RCC, the VHL gene is inactivated by mutation or hypermethylation. The resultant loss of functional pVHL means that HIF-α accumulates, even in the presence of normal oxygen. HIF translocates to the nucleus and constitutively activates the transcription of hundreds of hypoxia-response genes, most notably vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), erythropoietin (EPO), platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF), and transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β). This drives the highly vascular, aggressive tumor growth and the associated paraneoplastic syndromes.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A patient in their 60s or 70s. The classic triad of flank pain, a palpable flank mass, and hematuria is present in only 10% of cases and denotes advanced disease. Today, the vast majority of RCCs are discovered incidentally on an abdominal ultrasound or CT scan performed for another reason. Paraneoplastic syndromes are classic: hypercalcemia (from PTHrP production), erythrocytosis (from EPO overproduction), hypertension (from renin overproduction), and Stauffer syndrome (non-metastatic hepatic dysfunction that resolves after tumor removal).</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> An enhancing solid renal mass on contrast-enhanced CT, with clear cells on histology showing the characteristic rich lipid and glycogen content giving a &quot;golden yellow&quot; color on gross pathology.</li></ul><h3 id=\"17-urinary-tract-infection-uti\">17. Urinary Tract Infection (UTI)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Uropathogenic <em>Escherichia coli</em> (UPEC), which accounts for 80% of uncomplicated UTIs, originates from the patient&#39;s own fecal flora and colonizes the periurethral area, ascends the urethra into the bladder. UPEC expresses filamentous adhesins called Type 1 fimbriae and P-pili, which bind to specific glycolipid receptors on the urothelial cells, enabling attachment and preventing the bacteria from being washed away by the urine stream. The bacteria invade the urothelial cells, where they can replicate and form biofilm-like intracellular bacterial communities. The host mounts an intense neutrophilic inflammatory response, resulting in pyuria (WBCs in the urine), the cardinal sign.</li></ul><h3 id=\"typical-presentation\">Typical Presentation:</h3><ul><li><em>Uncomplicated Cystitis:</em> A young, sexually active woman presenting with the acute onset of dysuria, urinary frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain. Urine is cloudy and malodorous. Fever is absent.</li><li><em>Acute Pyelonephritis:</em> An infected kidney. The patient is systemically unwell with high fever, rigors (shaking chills), nausea, and vomiting. Severe flank pain and marked CVA tenderness are present. Urinalysis shows pyuria with WBC casts.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Pyuria and bacteriuria on urine culture in a patient with classic dysuria and frequency (cystitis) or CVA tenderness and fever (pyelonephritis).</li></ul><h3 id=\"18-nephrolithiasis-kidney-stones\">18. Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Stone formation is a complex crystallization process. Urine is a metastable solution of mineral salts, kept from crystallizing by the presence of inhibitors such as citrate, magnesium, and macromolecules like osteopontin. Stone formation occurs when the urine becomes supersaturated with a stone-forming salt. This can happen from low urine volume (dehydration is the greatest risk factor), excessive excretion of the solute (hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, cystinuria), an abnormal urine pH (uric acid stones form in persistently acidic urine, pH &lt;5.5; calcium phosphate and struvite stones form in alkaline urine), or a deficiency of inhibitors (hypocitraturia). The crystal nuclei grow and aggregate into a macroscopic stone.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A patient (more commonly male, peak age 20-40) with the sudden onset of severe, paroxysmal, colicky flank pain radiating to the groin and testicle/labium, associated with restlessness, nausea, and vomiting. The patient cannot find a comfortable position. Urinalysis typically shows microscopic or macroscopic hematuria. Non-contrast CT is the gold standard imaging, showing the stone.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Acute, severe, colicky flank pain with radiation to the groin, with the patient in extreme distress, and the presence of hematuria.</li></ul><h3 id=\"19-renal-artery-stenosis-ras\">19. Renal Artery Stenosis (RAS)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Narrowing of one or both renal arteries. The two major causes are <strong>atherosclerosis</strong> (90% of cases, in older adults with risk factors, affecting the ostium and proximal third of the main renal artery) and <strong>fibromuscular dysplasia</strong> (FMD, 10%, in young women, affecting the distal two-thirds with a characteristic &quot;string of beads&quot; appearance on angiography). The stenosis creates a hemodynamic pressure drop across the lesion. The reduced perfusion pressure in the post-stenotic segment is sensed by the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which activates the RAAS, causing severe, renin-dependent hypertension.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> A young woman with sudden onset hypertension (suggests FMD) or an older patient with refractory hypertension (requiring &gt;3 drugs), sudden &quot;flash&quot; pulmonary edema (Pickering syndrome), an unexplained unilateral small kidney on ultrasound, or an acute rise in creatinine of &gt;30% after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB.</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> Unexplained asymmetric kidney size, a renal artery bruit on auscultation, and the finding of hemodynamically significant stenosis on Doppler ultrasound or MRA/CTA.</li></ul><h3 id=\"20-myeloma-cast-nephropathy-myeloma-kidney\">20. Myeloma Cast Nephropathy (Myeloma Kidney)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> In multiple myeloma, the malignant plasma cells produce a vast excess of monoclonal immunoglobulin free light chains (FLCs). These FLCs are freely filtered by the glomerulus. In the proximal tubule, they are taken up by the megalin-cubilin endocytic receptor complex. The excessive endocytosis of FLCs is directly toxic to the tubular epithelium, causing acute tubular injury and necrosis. In addition, when the filtered load of FLCs exceeds the proximal tubular reabsorptive capacity, they arrive in the distal nephron. In the Tamm-Horsfall protein-rich environment of the thick ascending limb, the light chains bind to a specific peptide sequence on Tamm-Horsfall protein. They co-aggregate and precipitate to form dense, fractured, eosinophilic casts that obstruct the tubular lumen. This is the most common cause of AKI in multiple myeloma.</li><li><strong>Typical Presentation:</strong> An elderly patient (&gt;60 years) with known or newly diagnosed multiple myeloma (bone pain, anemia, hypercalcemia), presenting with AKI. The urine dipstick is deceptively negative for protein (since the reagent pad is sensitive to albumin, not light chains), but a 24-hour urine or UPEP/immunofixation reveals the presence of Bence-Jones proteins (monoclonal light chains).</li><li><strong>Key Distinguishing Feature:</strong> The combination of AKI, a negative urine dipstick for protein (albumin), and the detection of Bence-Jones protein in the urine.</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"seventh-emergency-conditions-of-the-renalurinary-system","عنوان":"Seventh: Emergency Conditions of the Renal/Urinary System","html":"<h3 id=\"1-life-threatening-hyperkalemia\">1. Life-Threatening Hyperkalemia</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> The kidney&#39;s ability to excrete potassium is severely compromised. Hyperkalemia results from a combination of impaired renal excretion (CKD, ESRD, AKI, drugs like ACEi/ARBs, MRA, NSAIDs, potassium-sparing diuretics) and a shift of K⁺ from cells to the ECF (metabolic acidosis, tissue breakdown from rhabdomyolysis or tumor lysis syndrome, insulin deficiency).</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> Muscle weakness, flaccid paralysis, palpitations, syncope. <strong>ECG changes</strong> are the life-threatening emergency: progression from peaked, tented T waves → prolonged PR interval, loss of P waves → widened QRS complex → the terminal &quot;sine wave&quot; pattern → ventricular fibrillation or asystole.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> Immediate stat serum K⁺ level and an urgent 12-lead ECG for ANY patient with renal disease and weakness or palpitations. A serum K⁺ &gt;6.5 mEq/L is a medical emergency. However, ANY K⁺ level associated with ECG changes is equally catastrophic and demands immediate action.</li></ul><h3 id=\"2-rapidly-progressive-glomerulonephritis-rpgn\">2. Rapidly Progressive Glomerulonephritis (RPGN)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Severe glomerular injury (from anti-GBM disease, ANCA vasculitis, severe lupus nephritis) causes rupture of the glomerular capillaries, allowing fibrin and inflammatory cells to spill into Bowman&#39;s space. The proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and macrophages forms cellular crescents that fill and obliterate the urinary space.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> A patient who develops an acute rise in serum creatinine over days to a few weeks, accompanied by a very active urine sediment showing <strong>dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts</strong>. This is the hallmark. Systemic symptoms of vasculitis or hemoptysis may be present.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> The finding of RBC casts on urine microscopy in a patient with AKI is a renal emergency. It demands immediate nephrology consultation and serological testing for ANCA, anti-GBM, ANA, and complement, followed by an urgent renal biopsy. If left untreated for days, the cellular crescents will fibrose into irreversible fibrous crescents, and the patient will progress to permanent, irreversible dialysis-dependent kidney failure.</li></ul><h3 id=\"3-malignant-hypertension-with-thrombotic-microangiopathy-tma\">3. Malignant Hypertension with Thrombotic Microangiopathy (TMA)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> A hypertensive emergency (often SBP &gt;220 mmHg or DBP &gt;130 mmHg) with acute, ongoing target-organ damage to the kidneys. The severely elevated pressure causes mechanical, barotrauma-induced endothelial injury to the afferent arterioles and glomerular capillaries. This triggers platelet adhesion and the formation of fibrin microthrombi within the glomeruli (malignant nephrosclerosis). The destruction of red blood cells as they traverse the fibrin-laden microvasculature produces schistocytes on the peripheral blood smear and a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> Severely elevated BP, severe headache, visual disturbances (blurred vision, scotomas), encephalopathy (confusion, seizures), and AKI. Funduscopy reveals the pathognomonic <strong>bilateral papilledema</strong>, along with retinal hemorrhages and hard exudates.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> Fundoscopy showing bilateral papilledema in a patient with a markedly elevated blood pressure and AKI is diagnostic. This requires a controlled, gradual reduction in blood pressure (no more than 25% reduction in the first few hours) in an intensive care setting, typically using intravenous agents like labetalol, nicardipine, or clevidipine to prevent watershed cerebral ischemia.</li></ul><h3 id=\"4-obstructive-uropathy-with-anuria\">4. Obstructive Uropathy with Anuria</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Complete obstruction of urine flow from both kidneys, or from a solitary functioning kidney, or complete bladder outlet obstruction. The back-pressure is transmitted to Bowman&#39;s space, reducing or abolishing the net filtration pressure.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> The sudden onset of <strong>anuria</strong> (urine output &lt;100 mL/24 hours). This is virtually pathognomonic for complete bilateral ureteric or complete bladder outlet obstruction.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> Anuria demands an immediate bedside bladder scan or a straight (&quot;in-and-out&quot;) urinary catheterization to measure post-void residual urine volume. If the bladder is empty, a stat renal ultrasound is the next step to look for bilateral hydronephrosis. If hydronephrosis is present, urgent decompression (via percutaneous nephrostomy tubes or retrograde ureteral stents) is mandatory to salvage renal function.</li></ul><h3 id=\"5-acute-pulmonary-edema-from-volume-overload\">5. Acute Pulmonary Edema from Volume Overload</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> In oliguric or anuric AKI or ESRD, the kidney cannot excrete the ingested sodium and water. The extracellular fluid volume expands rapidly. The left ventricular filling pressure exceeds the threshold for pulmonary capillary transudation, and fluid floods the pulmonary interstitium and alveoli.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> Sudden, severe dyspnea, orthopnea (inability to lie flat), paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (waking from sleep gasping for air), and coughing up frothy, pink-tinged sputum.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> Physical examination reveals a patient in severe respiratory distress, sitting bolt upright, using accessory muscles of respiration. Auscultation reveals diffuse, bilateral coarse crackles (&quot;bubbling&quot; rales) throughout the lung fields. An S3 gallop is heard. The JVP is markedly elevated. A chest X-ray shows the classic pattern of central, perihilar, &quot;bat-wing&quot; alveolar edema. This requires emergency management: high-flow oxygen, non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (CPAP/BiPAP), intravenous loop diuretics, and urgent hemodialysis for fluid removal if the patient is oliguric.</li></ul><h3 id=\"6-uremic-pericarditis\">6. Uremic Pericarditis</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Fibrinous inflammation of the pericardium due to high levels of uremic toxins in the pericardial fluid. A pericardial effusion may develop, which can be hemorrhagic due to uremic platelet dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> New-onset, sharp, pleuritic chest pain that is relieved by leaning forward and worsened by lying supine, in a patient with advanced, untreated uremia.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> The pathognomonic auscultatory finding is a <strong>pericardial friction rub</strong>. The ECG shows diffuse ST-segment elevation. The major, feared complication is cardiac tamponade, presenting with hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and an elevated JVP (Beck&#39;s triad). This requires urgent initiation of daily, intensive hemodialysis without heparin to avoid bleeding into the pericardial space.</li></ul><h3 id=\"7-calciphylaxis-calcific-uremic-arteriolopathy\">7. Calciphylaxis (Calcific Uremic Arteriolopathy)</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> Medial calcification and thrombotic occlusion of the dermal and subcutaneous arterioles, leading to critical skin ischemia and necrosis. The risk factors are advanced CKD (especially dialysis patients), hyperparathyroidism, elevated calcium-phosphate product, and, most critically, the use of warfarin (which inhibits the vitamin K-dependent matrix Gla protein, a powerful endogenous inhibitor of vascular calcification).</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> The development of exquisitely painful, livedoid, purpuric, and indurated plaques, typically on the thighs, abdomen, and buttocks, which rapidly progress to black, necrotic, non-healing, and often superinfected eschars. The pain is characteristically severe, out of proportion to the skin findings, and refractory to opioids.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> A high index of suspicion in a dialysis patient with painful skin lesions. The diagnosis is clinical and confirmed by a deep skin punch biopsy. It carries a very high mortality rate (&gt;50-80% at 1 year), with the most common cause of death being sepsis from the necrotic wounds.</li></ul><h3 id=\"8-emphysematous-pyelonephritis\">8. Emphysematous Pyelonephritis</h3><ul><li><strong>Pathogenesis:</strong> A life-threatening, gas-forming, necrotizing infection of the renal parenchyma, almost exclusively in patients with poorly controlled diabetes. The hyperglycemic, poorly perfused tissue provides an ideal anaerobic environment. Gram-negative bacteria (especially <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>) ferment glucose, producing carbon dioxide and hydrogen gas, which dissects through the renal parenchyma and into the collecting system.</li><li><strong>Red Flags:</strong> A diabetic patient presenting with severe pyelonephritis (high fever, CVA tenderness, vomiting) who is acutely deteriorating, with signs of sepsis and septic shock.</li><li><strong>Detection:</strong> The diagnosis is made by a non-contrast CT scan of the abdomen, which shows gas within the renal parenchyma or collecting system. It requires immediate, aggressive, broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and, in many cases, urgent percutaneous drainage or emergency nephrectomy.</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"eighth-pharmacological-drug-categories-in-nephrology","عنوان":"Eighth: Pharmacological Drug Categories in Nephrology","html":"<p>A definitive list of the drug categories working in the renal system.</p><h3 id=\"diuretics\">Diuretics:</h3><ul><li><em>Loop Diuretics (Furosemide, Bumetanide, Torsemide):</em> Inhibit Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter (NKCC2) in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle.</li><li><em>Thiazide Diuretics (Hydrochlorothiazide, Chlorthalidone):</em> Inhibit Na⁺-Cl⁻ cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule.</li><li><em>Potassium-Sparing Diuretics:</em> Amiloride (blocks ENaC in collecting duct). Spironolactone and Eplerenone (block mineralocorticoid receptor in collecting duct).</li><li><em>Osmotic Diuretics (Mannitol):</em> Filtered but not reabsorbed, exerting an osmotic force that traps water in the tubular lumen.</li></ul><h3 id=\"inhibitors-of-the-renin-angiotensin-aldosterone-system-raas-blockers\">Inhibitors of the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS Blockers):</h3><ul><li><em>ACE Inhibitors (Lisinopril, Ramipril):</em> Inhibit angiotensin-converting enzyme.</li><li><em>ARBs (Losartan, Valsartan):</em> Block the angiotensin II Type 1 receptor.</li><li><em>ARNI (Sacubitril/Valsartan):</em> Neprilysin inhibitor and ARB combination.</li><li><em>Non-Steroidal MRA (Finerenone):</em> Selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>SGLT2 Inhibitors (Dapagliflozin, Empagliflozin):</strong> Inhibit the sodium-glucose cotransporter in the proximal tubule, reducing glomerular hyperfiltration and providing profound renoprotection.</li><li><strong>Vasopressin Receptor Antagonists (Vaptans: Tolvaptan):</strong> Competitive antagonist of the ADH V2 receptor, blocking water reabsorption in the collecting duct, used for hyponatremia and ADPKD.</li><li><strong>Potassium Binders (Sodium Zirconium Cyclosilicate, Patiromer):</strong> Non-absorbed cation exchange compounds that bind potassium in the GI tract in exchange for sodium or calcium, increasing fecal excretion.</li><li><strong>Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs: Epoetin Alfa, Darbepoetin Alfa):</strong> Recombinant human erythropoietin that stimulates red blood cell production in the bone marrow.</li><li><strong>HIF Prolyl Hydroxylase Inhibitors (Roxadustat):</strong> Stabilize HIF transcription factor, leading to coordinated endogenous EPO production and improved iron mobilization.</li><li><strong>Phosphate Binders (Calcium Acetate, Sevelamer, Lanthanum):</strong> Bind dietary phosphate in the gut, preventing its absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcimimetics (Cinacalcet):</strong> Allosterically activate the calcium-sensing receptor on the parathyroid gland, suppressing PTH secretion.</li><li><strong>Immunosuppressants (Glucocorticoids, Mycophenolate Mofetil, Cyclophosphamide, Rituximab):</strong> Target specific components of the immune system to treat immune-mediated glomerulonephritides.</li><li><strong>Anti-complement Therapy (Eculizumab):</strong> Monoclonal antibody that binds to C5, preventing the formation of the membrane attack complex, used in aHUS.</li></ul><p><strong>Sources:</strong></p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a> Simerville JA, Maxted WC, Pahira JJ. Urinalysis: a comprehensive review. <em>Am Fam Physician</em>. 2005;71<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a>:1153-1162.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a> Fogazzi GB, Ponticelli C, Ritz E. The Urinary Sediment: An Integrated View. 2nd ed. Oxford University Press; 1999.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a> Vivarelli M, Massella L, Ruggiero B, Emma F. Minimal Change Disease. <em>Clin J Am Soc Nephrol</em>. 2017;12<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a>:332-345. doi:10.2215/CJN.05000516</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a> Schrier RW. Body fluid volume regulation in health and disease: a unifying hypothesis. <em>Ann Intern Med</em>. 1990;113<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a>:155-159.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-5\" data-مصادر=\"5\">[5]</a> Siddall EC, Radhakrishnan J. The pathophysiology of edema formation in the nephrotic syndrome. <em>Kidney Int</em>. 2012;82<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a>:635-642. doi:10.1038/ki.2012.180</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a> Anderson RJ, Schrier RW. Acute renal failure. In: Schrier RW, ed. <em>Diseases of the Kidney and Urinary Tract</em>. 8th ed. Lippincott Williams &amp; Wilkins; 2007.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\">[7]</a> Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) CKD Work Group. KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease. <em>Kidney Int</em>. 2024;105(4S):S117-S314.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a> Weissenborn K. Neurologic manifestations of uremia. In: Depner TA, ed. <em>Prescribing Hemodialysis: A Guide to Urea Kinetic Modeling</em>. Springer; 2011.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a> Rehman KA, Betancor J, Xu B, et al. Uremic pericarditis and pericardial effusion in end-stage renal disease. <em>J Am Coll Cardiol</em>. 2017;69<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a>:2132. doi:10.1016/j.jacc.2016.12.035</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a> Luciano RL, Moeckel GW. Update on the Native Kidney Biopsy: Core Curriculum 2019. <em>Am J Kidney Dis</em>. 2019;73<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a>:404-415. doi:10.1053/j.ajkd.2018.10.011</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a> Levey AS, Eckardt KU, Dorman NM, et al. Nomenclature for kidney function and disease: report of a Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) Consensus Conference. <em>Kidney Int</em>. 2020;97<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a>:1117-1129. doi:10.1016/j.kint.2020.02.010</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a> Bonventre JV, Yang L. Cellular pathophysiology of ischemic acute kidney injury. <em>J Clin Invest</em>. 2011;121<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a>:4210-4221. doi:10.1172/JCI45161</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a> Rodríguez-Iturbe B, Musser JM. The current state of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. <em>J Am Soc Nephrol</em>. 2008;19<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a>:1855-1864. doi:10.1681/ASN.2008010092</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\">[14]</a> Wyatt RJ, Julian BA. IgA nephropathy. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2013;368<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-25\" data-مصادر=\"25\">[25]</a>:2402-2414. doi:10.1056/NEJMra1206793</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\">[15]</a> Rosenberg AZ, Kopp JB. Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis. <em>Clin J Am Soc Nephrol</em>. 2017;12<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a>:502-517. doi:10.2215/CJN.05960616</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-16\" data-مصادر=\"16\">[16]</a> Beck LH Jr, Bonegio RG, Lambeau G, et al. M-type phospholipase A2 receptor as target antigen in idiopathic membranous nephropathy. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2009;361<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a>:11-21. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa0810457</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-17\" data-مصادر=\"17\">[17]</a> Anders HJ, Saxena R, Zhao MH, et al. Lupus nephritis. <em>Nat Rev Dis Primers</em>. 2020;6<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a>:7. doi:10.1038/s41572-019-0141-9</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-18\" data-مصادر=\"18\">[18]</a> Jennette JC, Nachman PH. ANCA Glomerulonephritis and Vasculitis. <em>Clin J Am Soc Nephrol</em>. 2017;12<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a>:1680-1691. doi:10.2215/CJN.02500317</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-19\" data-مصادر=\"19\">[19]</a> Torres VE, Harris PC, Pirson Y. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. <em>Lancet</em>. 2007;369<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9569\" data-مصادر=\"9569\">[9569]</a>:1287-1301. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-07\" data-مصادر=\"07\">[07]</a>60601-1</p><div class=\"كتلة تحقّق\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Verification footer</p><p><strong>Guidelines used:</strong> KDIGO is the governing body for kidney disease; the file itself was not re-derived from it [PK].</p><p><strong>Generated:</strong> 12 September 2026.</p><p><strong>Could not verify — read this before quoting:</strong> the source document carries numbered citation markers but <strong>no bibliography</strong>. The numbers resolve to nothing, and every quantitative value here is <strong><button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></strong> until a source is attached.</p><p><strong>Confirm before clinical application:</strong> treat this file as revision notes. For any treatment decision go to the primary guideline, not to this page.</p></div>"}],"مصادر":[]});
